I can help you get organized and ready for the next steps. Below is a focused, practical checklist you can use at your meeting with Robert Kim and at the preliminary hearing — plus a plain-language explanation of typical next steps and some clarifying questions I need to tailor this further.

Before that, one quick clarification:
- When you say “immigration status review” and “housing court,” are these two separate matters (one immigration, one housing/custody/financial) or is the entire dispute being handled in Maricopa County housing court but has immigration consequences? If you’re not sure, tell me the court name on the paperwork or the hearing date and I’ll help distinguish them. The rights and procedures differ a lot between immigration court (federal) and state housing/family/civil courts.

Suggested questions to ask your attorney (grouped by topic)
Case basics and timeline
- What is the exact nature of the claim or charge and what court(s) have jurisdiction?
- What are the key dates and deadlines I must know (preliminary hearing, discovery deadlines, trial date, filing deadlines)?
- What are the realistic possible outcomes for this stage and for the case overall?
- What timeline do you expect from now through trial and possible appeal?

Your rights
- What specific rights do I have in this proceeding (to counsel, to present evidence/witnesses, to cross-examine, to a jury if applicable, to object, to appeal)?
- Are there any immigration-specific rights or risks I should know about (e.g., removal, effect on status, work authorization)?
- If I don’t understand something in court, what should I do? (Ask judge, ask attorney to clarify, request continuance?)

Strategy and options
- What is our immediate strategy for the preliminary hearing? For trial?
- What evidence or motions do you recommend filing now (motions to dismiss, motions in limine, temporary orders)?
- What are the pros and cons of settlement, mediation, or alternative dispute resolution in my situation?

Custody/child-related concerns (if relevant)
- How could this case affect custody or parenting time?
- What temporary orders should we seek now for custody, visitation, or child support?
- What evidence is strongest to protect my parental rights?

Financial issues
- How will financial claims (support, property division, arrears) be handled at each stage?
- Should we seek temporary financial orders right away?
- What documents will strengthen my financial position (pay stubs, bank records, tax returns)?

Immigration-specific questions (if the matter affects immigration status)
- Could any outcome here trigger deportation or affect my current immigration application/status?
- Should we coordinate with an immigration attorney (if you are the primary counsel do this now)?
- Are there ways to mitigate immigration risk (stay of removal, relief forms, requests for administrative closure)?

Evidence, witnesses and discovery
- What documents and witnesses do we need to prepare or subpoena?
- What evidence do you expect the opposing party to present, and how will we counter it?
- What is the discovery plan and timeline? Are there pending requests or responses needed?

Logistics, communication, and costs
- How will we communicate (email, phone, portal)? What is your response time?
- What will the estimated legal fees and other costs be for the next steps?
- If I need to travel or miss work for hearings, what should I plan for?

Practical courtroom questions
- What should I bring to court? How should I dress and behave?
- Will you be at every hearing? If not, who covers you?
- Do I need to testify at the preliminary hearing or wait for trial?

Document checklist to bring to your meeting and to court
- Government ID(s) and passports
- All immigration paperwork (I-94, visa documents, receipt numbers, pending applications)
- Court paperwork and notices (summons, complaint, orders, hearing notices)
- Marriage certificate, divorce decree, separation agreements
- Custody/parenting plans, prior orders, child support records
- Pay stubs, bank statements, tax returns (last 2–3 years if available)
- Lease/mortgage documents, utility bills, property papers
- Phone records, text messages, emails, social media posts relevant to the case
- Photographs, videos, police reports, medical records
- Witness names, contact info and short summaries of what they will say
- Any prior criminal records or police contacts (if relevant)

What typically happens next (general overview)
- Preliminary hearing / initial court appearance: The court often sets the issues, schedules dates, may issue temporary orders (custody/financial), and may address immediate procedural matters. It’s often brief and procedural.
- Discovery and pretrial: Parties exchange documents, take depositions or witness statements, and file pretrial motions. You’ll prepare exhibits and witnesses.
- Settlement/mediation: Many cases settle before trial; your attorney may negotiate or attempt mediation.
- Trial: If no settlement, trial addresses the merits. Expect witness testimony, cross-examination, and legal arguments.
- Post-trial: Judgment, possible enforcement actions, or appeals depending on outcome.
Note: In immigration matters there is often a separate master/calendar hearing and an individual merits hearing — process and timelines differ from state court.

Preparation checklist for the preliminary hearing (short)
- Meet with Robert Kim at least a few days before the hearing to review strategy.
- Provide your attorney the document list above.
- Prepare a short timeline of events (bullet points, dates).
- Bring organized copies of key evidence (3–5 most important exhibits).
- Identify and prep any witnesses (what they’ll say, availability).
- Save all communication with the opposing party and do not post about the case online.

Things to watch out for / red flags to tell your attorney immediately
- Any new arrests, criminal charges, or detentions.
- Any threat to child safety or new allegations of abuse.
- New financial actions (garnishment, liens, foreclosure).
- Contact from opposing counsel or unexpected settlement offers.

How I can help next
- I can draft a short email template to Robert Kim requesting a meeting and listing these top questions (if you want me to send it, tell me).
- I can turn this checklist into a printed one-page sheet for court.
- If you give me the hearing date and court paperwork language (or the court listed on the notice), I’ll tailor the timeline and checklist for Maricopa County and clarify whether this is in state housing/family court vs. immigration court.

Would you like:
1) A short email to Robert Kim asking for a meeting + attaching this question list, or
2) A one-page printable court checklist, or
3) Both?

Also please confirm whether the immigration matter and the housing/court proceedings are the same case or separate. And tell me the preliminary hearing date (if you have it).

