- Bring a concise timeline of events (dates, short descriptions) and copies of key documents.
- Prepare a short, factual statement of what you want the judge to do (temporary custody, stay in home, protection).
- Avoid making emotional or speculative statements in court — stick to facts.
- Coordinate witness availability and make sure witnesses know what to expect.
- Ask your attorney for a quick rehearsal of what you’ll say.

Practical tips
- Confirm exactly which court and judge are handling the case and their local procedures (virtual vs. in-person, rules on exhibits).
- Make sure your attorney has copies of all documents at least a few days before hearings.
- Keep a running file (paper or digital) with dated entries of all incidents and communications.

