I can help with that. I’m not a lawyer, but I can help you prepare focused questions and a checklist so you make the most of your time with Robert Kim and feel ready for the next steps. Below are (1) top-priority questions to ask at your next meeting, (2) a longer categorized list you can use as an agenda, (3) a document checklist, (4) a typical case timeline so you know what to expect, and (5) practical tips and reminders. If you want, I can also draft an email or a one-page meeting summary you can bring to the appointment.

1) Quick disclaimer
- I can’t give legal advice. Use these to guide your conversation with your attorney. If you want, I’ll tailor the list once you tell me the hearing date and more specifics (e.g., exact immigration status, whether children are involved, what type of housing court matter this is).

2) Top 10 questions to ask your attorney (priorities for the next meeting)
1. What is the purpose of the upcoming preliminary hearing and what should I expect on that day?  
2. How does this housing/court matter interact with my immigration status (if at all)? Could anything here trigger immigration consequences?  
3. What are my immediate rights and obligations right now (do I need to appear anywhere, pay anything, or avoid contact with anyone)?  
4. What defenses or legal strategies do you recommend given the facts of my case?  
5. What evidence and documents will best support my position (custody, finances, tenancy, communications)?  
6. Are there deadlines I must meet (filings, discovery responses, disclosures)? What are they?  
7. Who will represent me at the preliminary hearing and at trial (you, another attorney, or will you bring co-counsel)?  
8. What are the realistic possible outcomes (including best-case, most likely, and worst-case), and how would each affect my immigration status and custody/finances?  
9. What’s the estimated timeline, steps, and likely dates (next hearings, discovery, mediation, trial)?  
10. What will this cost (fees, court costs, expert witness fees) and are there ways to reduce costs (settlement, mediation, phased approach)?

3) Expanded question list (by topic)
A. Case basics & strategy
- What is the overall strategy and why?  
- Are there alternative dispute resolution options (mediation, settlement conference) you recommend?  
- Should we seek a continuance/postponement for any reason?

B. Immigration-specific
- Could findings in housing/family court be used in immigration proceedings?  
- Are there criminal or immigration bars I should be worried about (crime-related grounds, fraud, public charge, etc.)?  
- Do I need to notify immigration authorities or can we/should we avoid that?  
- If removal proceedings are possible, what proactive steps should we take now?

C. Custody & finances
- How will temporary court orders affect custody, visitation, or child support?  
- How do my immigration status/ability to travel affect custody arrangements?  
- What financial documents should I prepare (bank statements, tax returns, pay stubs, lease, mortgage, bills)?

D. Evidence & witnesses
- Which witnesses would be most helpful and how should we prepare them?  
- How to preserve evidence (screenshots, text messages, emails, photos)?  
- Are there any documents I should NOT sign or share without your review?

E. Discovery, motions & trial prep
- What discovery will be requested and how long does it take?  
- Are pretrial motions likely (e.g., motions to dismiss, exclude evidence)?  
- How long will trial prep take and will any experts be needed?

F. Logistics & communication
- How will we communicate (email, phone, client portal)? Who is my point of contact?  
- What do you need from me before the hearing (documents, signed forms, statements)?

4) Document checklist to bring to your attorney meeting
- Government ID/passport, immigration documents (I-94, visa, green card, naturalization paperwork, pending applications)  
- Any official notices you’ve received (court notices, landlord notices, immigration notices)  
- Court filings and prior orders relating to the case (pleadings, restraining orders, custody orders)  
- Lease or mortgage documents and rental/utility payment records  
- Bank statements, pay stubs, tax returns (last 2–3 years if available)  
- Text messages, emails, social media posts relevant to the dispute (print or export them)  
- Photos, videos, recordings, witness contact info and short summary of what each can attest to  
- Child custody related docs: school records, medical records, childcare expenses  
- Any police reports, arrests, or criminal case documents (if relevant)

5) Typical next steps & timeline (general overview)
- Preliminary hearing: court sets schedule, addresses emergency requests, may issue temporary orders. Purpose is often case management, not a full trial.  
- Discovery phase: exchange of documents, witness lists, depositions (if applicable).  
- Motions/hearings: either side may file motions to resolve legal issues pretrial (e.g., excluding evidence, dismissing claims).  
- Settlement talks/mediation: often occurs before trial; many cases resolve here.  
- Pretrial conference: judge reviews readiness and confirms logistics for trial.  
- Trial: evidence and witness testimony; judge or jury decides.  
- Post-trial: judgment enforcement, appeals (if applicable), and any orders that could affect immigration status will need coordination.  
Note: timelines vary widely by court and case complexity — ask Robert for local Maricopa County timing.

6) Your rights and important do’s & don’ts
- Do not sign settlement agreements, custody agreements, or stipulations without attorney review.  
- Preserve evidence immediately (save copies of everything, export digital communications).  
- Avoid posting details about the case on social media. Opposing counsel may use it.  
- Know whether you have the right to appointed counsel (in immigration court you generally do not; in some criminal matters you do).  
- Ask about confidentiality and whether communications with your attorney are protected.  
- If questioned by police or ICE, know your rights — ask your lawyer what to do. (If you’re unsure, politely decline to answer without counsel.)

7) Questions I have for you so I can tailor this further
- What is your exact immigration status (e.g., visa type, pending application, green card holder, asylum applicant)?  
- What is the date of the preliminary hearing?  
- Are there children involved (ages)? Is custody already court-ordered?  
- Has any party been arrested or are there criminal proceedings?  
- Is Robert Kim your immigration lawyer, family lawyer, or representing you in housing court?

8) Offer to help
- I can draft a concise email or meeting agenda you can send to Robert Kim listing these questions and attaching documents.  
- I can also create a single-page “what to say at the hearing” script or a checklist to bring with you.

Tell me which of the follow-up options you want:
- I’ll draft an email to Robert Kim with the top questions and attach a document checklist.  
- I’ll create a 1-page meeting sheet you can print and bring to the hearing.  
- Or tell me the hearing date and your immigration status and I’ll customize the questions and timeline.

