Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help you get organized and ready. First: one quick clarification — you mentioned both “immigration status review” and “housing court.” Are these two separate proceedings (an immigration matter in immigration court and a housing/court eviction or family-related hearing in Maricopa County), or is the entire matter in housing court? That will change some of the specific questions and timeline items. If you’re not sure, tell me what the upcoming hearing notice says (court name, case type, date) and I can tailor the list.

Below is a practical checklist you can use right away: questions to ask your attorney, documents to gather, typical next steps, and practical preparation tips.

Questions to ask your attorney
- Case basics and jurisdiction
  - Which court is this in (immigration court, family court, housing court, etc.) and why? How does jurisdiction affect my rights and options?
  - What exactly will happen at the preliminary hearing? What issues will the judge decide at that hearing?
  - What are important upcoming deadlines I must not miss?

- Immigration-specific
  - How does this proceeding affect my immigration status (risk of removal, denial of benefits, effect on adjustment of status, etc.)?
  - Are there immigration remedies available to me (adjustment, cancellation, VAWA, U visa, asylum, waivers)? Which are realistic in my situation?
  - Will pursuing/losing this case trigger enforcement action, detention, or removal proceedings?
  - Do I need to apply for or renew work authorization, and will participating in this case affect that?

- Custody/financial and family-law overlap
  - How could custody or financial orders from this case affect my immigration situation (or vice versa)?
  - Can temporary custody, child support, or spousal support orders be requested at the preliminary hearing?
  - Are there immediate protections (orders of protection, emergency custody) I should consider?

- Evidence, witnesses, and discovery
  - What evidence will be strongest for my case? What documents or records do you need from me?
  - Who should we list as witnesses? Do any third parties need to be subpoenaed?
  - What discovery is available and what is the timeline for exchanging documents?

- Strategy and likely outcomes
  - What are the possible outcomes and the likelihood of each (best case, realistic, worst case)?
  - What’s our litigation strategy? Are settlement or alternative dispute resolution options appropriate?
  - If we lose, what are my appeal options and timelines?

- Logistics, communication, and fees
  - Who will represent me at each hearing (attorney, co-counsel, paralegal)? Will you attend the preliminary hearing?
  - How and when should I communicate with you (email, phone)? Who do I contact in an emergency?
  - What are the fees and estimated additional costs (court fees, expert witnesses, translations, travel)?
  - Are communications between us fully confidential (privilege/limits)?

- Safety and contact with opposing party
  - Should I avoid contacting my ex-spouse directly? What do we do about exchanges with them (custody, living arrangements)?
  - If there is a history of domestic violence, what protections or specialized remedies exist?

Documents and information to gather
- Government IDs and immigration paperwork: passport, green card, visas, I-94, past applications or notices, any immigration court documents
- Court documents and notices you’ve received (summons, complaint, hearing notices)
- Marriage certificate, divorce decree (if any), separation agreements
- Custody orders, parenting plans, child-related records (school, medical)
- Financial records: bank statements, tax returns (last 2–3 years), pay stubs, bills, proof of contributions to household
- Lease/mortgage documents, utility bills, proof of residency
- Communications: texts, emails, social media messages relevant to the dispute
- Police reports, restraining orders, child welfare or CPS reports if applicable
- Witness contact info and short summary of what each can testify about
- A written timeline of events (dates, places, key interactions) — very helpful for your attorney

Typical next steps and timeline (general)
- Preliminary hearing / status conference: narrow issues, set schedule for discovery, possibly temporary orders
- Discovery and evidence gathering: exchange documents, request witness statements, subpoenas if needed
- Pretrial motions: motions to dismiss, evidentiary motions, motions for temporary relief
- Settlement discussions/mediation: many cases settle before trial if terms can be negotiated
- Trial: presentation of evidence/witnesses, judge issues decision
- Post-trial: enforce orders, or file appeals if appropriate
Note: timelines vary widely by jurisdiction and case type. Ask your attorney for the local schedule in Maricopa County.

Practical tips for the hearing and between now and then
- Create a concise timeline (one page) and share it with your attorney.
- Bring originals and copies of critical documents to every meeting and hearing.
- Don’t communicate with the opposing party about case issues without your attorney’s advice.
- Preserve electronic evidence: back up texts, screenshots with timestamps, and emails.
- Practice a short, factual statement of your position (two or three sentences) so you can summarize clearly in court or to your lawyer.
- If safety is a concern, raise it with your attorney immediately so protective steps can be taken.

What I can do to help right now
- Draft a clear list of the above questions into an email you can send to Robert Kim (I can draft it for you).
- Search your inbox for emails from “Robert Kim” or “Kim & Associates” and summarize recent messages or find the hearing notice.
- Draft a one-page timeline or an evidence checklist for you to fill out and give to your attorney.

Which of those would you like me to do next? If you want an email to your attorney, tell me whether you want it sent now or saved as a draft, and whether to copy anyone else. If you want me to look in your inbox for messages from Robert Kim, confirm and I’ll search for them.

