If you want, I can also draft/send this email to Robert Kim or save it as a draft — tell me which.

1) High-priority questions to ask Robert Kim
- Case basics and timeline
  - What exactly is the claim/issue filed against me (summary of allegations and relief sought)?
  - What is the date/time/location of the preliminary hearing, and what should I expect to happen there?
  - What are the key deadlines I need to know (discovery cutoffs, motion filing dates, trial date)?
- Immigration consequences
  - Could this housing/court proceeding affect my immigration status (deportation risk, impact on pending petitions, public charge, etc.)?
  - Should I consult an immigration specialist — do you recommend/know one who can coordinate with our team?
- Custody and financial exposure
  - What exposure do I have on custody and child-support/financial orders as a result of this case? How might temporary orders affect custody?
  - Can temporary orders be requested at the preliminary hearing (e.g., temporary custody, possession, support)?
- Evidence and witnesses
  - What evidence will be most important to our defense (documents, text messages, witnesses)?
  - Who should I identify as witnesses and what should they be asked to prepare?
- Strategy, settlement, and trial
  - What is our recommended strategy (defend, seek settlement, mediation)?
  - What are realistic best-case and worst-case outcomes?
  - How likely is this to go to trial and how long would trial preparation take?
- Procedural protections and rights
  - Do I have the right to a translator or interpreter at hearings?
  - Are there any restrictions on my testifying? What happens if I am asked questions that could affect my immigration status?
- Practical and administrative
  - What do you need from me right now (documents, contacts, timeline, payments)?
  - How will we communicate (email/phone) and how fast can I expect responses?
  - What are your fees and an estimate of additional costs if case proceeds to trial?

2) Documents and evidence to gather immediately
- Identification and immigration records: passport, visa, I-94, green card, naturalization paperwork, applications/petitions, work authorization, previous immigration correspondence.
- Court and case documents: any filings you've received, notices, previous court orders, summonses.
- Custody/child records: birth certificates, school records, child medical records, daycare communications, custody agreements, custody-related court orders.
- Financial records: bank statements, pay stubs, tax returns, lease or mortgage documents, rent receipts, bills, proof of support or expenses.
- Communications: text messages, emails, social media messages, voicemails relevant to dispute.
- Photographs / videos / logs: dated photos, timeline notes, spreadsheets summarizing events.
- Police reports or incident reports, restraining/protective orders, 911 calls.
- Witness list and contact details, with brief notes about what each witness can say.
- Any evidence of housing payments, repairs, damages, or landlord communications if housing-related.

3) What typically happens at a preliminary hearing and next steps
- Preliminary hearing purpose: confirm identity of parties, read allegations, potentially set temporary orders (possession, custody, support, or stay-away), set schedule for discovery and deadlines, and set or confirm trial date.
- Common immediate outcomes: case continued and scheduling order issued; temporary orders (short-term custody/possession) may be entered; judge may order mediation or settlement conference.
- Discovery phase: exchange of documents, written questions (interrogatories), depositions or sworn statements, subpoenas for records.
- Motions: either side may file motions (to dismiss, for protective orders, to exclude evidence, for temporary relief). Expect pretrial motions and hearings.
- Mediation/settlement: many cases try mediation before trial; settlement negotiations can occur at any point.
- Trial prep: witness prep, exhibit lists, pretrial conference, jury selection (if jury-triable), presentation of evidence at trial.
- Post-trial: judgment, enforcement, possible appeals, and consequences for immigration — coordinate with immigration counsel.

Typical timeline (general):
- Preliminary hearing: within weeks (confirm date with lawyer).
- Discovery and motion period: typically weeks-to-months depending on court load.
- Trial: often months after preliminary hearing; exact timing varies widely by court and case complexity.
