Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help you get prepared. I’m not a lawyer, so this isn’t legal advice — think of this as an organized checklist and suggested questions you can take to Robert Kim. Below are: (A) focused questions to ask your attorney, (B) documents and evidence to gather, (C) what typically happens next in a case like this (timeline / steps), and (D) immediate action items before the preliminary hearing.

A — Key questions to ask your attorney (Robert Kim)
- Case status and strategy
  - What is our overall strategy and likely outcomes for the preliminary hearing and possible trial?
  - What is the strength of the opposing party’s case and our best defenses?
  - Are there any immediate procedural steps we should take to improve our position?
- Immigration-specific concerns
  - How (if at all) could the housing court proceedings or any outcomes affect my immigration status or any pending immigration applications?
  - Could anything in this case trigger removal, inadmissibility, or other immigration consequences? If so, what defenses or mitigations exist?
  - Are there any immigration relief options I should consider (adjustment of status, cancellation, VAWA, U or T visas, waivers, etc.)?
  - Should we coordinate with an immigration specialist if needed?
- Custody and child-related issues
  - How will custody claims or temporary custody orders be handled? Can housing court rulings influence custody determinations?
  - What temporary custody, parenting time, or child support orders should we seek now?
  - If there are safety concerns, how do we request protective orders or emergency custody?
- Financial matters
  - How do financial issues (assets, debts, support payments) affect this case and what temporary financial relief can we request?
  - What documentation will be persuasive for spousal/child support, division of assets, or housing-related financial claims?
- Evidence, witnesses, and discovery
  - What specific evidence do you recommend we gather now?
  - Should we issue subpoenas, demand documents, or take depositions before the hearing?
  - Who should we list as witnesses and what will be expected of them?
- Hearing logistics and options
  - What should I expect at the preliminary hearing (format, time, likely decisions)?
  - Should I testify at the preliminary hearing or wait until trial?
  - Are settlement/mediation options likely and when is the best time to negotiate?
  - If we aren’t ready, can we ask for a continuance? What’s the risk?
- Costs and communication
  - What are the expected costs from now through trial? Billing rates, retainer, and likely additional expenses (experts, investigations)?
  - How will you communicate updates and how quickly can I contact you with questions?
- Next steps and deadlines
  - What immediate deadlines or filings should I be aware of?
  - What do you need from me before the preliminary hearing?

B — Documents and evidence to collect now
- Identity & immigration documents: passports, visas, green card, USCIS / ICE notices, any immigration filings and receipts (I-130, I-485, etc.).
- Court / case documents: any summons, complaints, notices from housing court, pleadings, hearing notices, prior orders.
- Housing records: lease/rental agreement, rent payment records, receipts, eviction notices, communications with landlord or ex-spouse about housing.
- Financial records: bank statements, pay stubs, tax returns (recent years), credit card statements, mortgage or rent ledgers, invoices, receipts for major expenditures.
- Support & custody records: child school records, medical records, custody schedules, proof of child support or spousal support payments or lack thereof.
- Communications: texts, emails, social media posts, written notices, recorded messages (if legally obtained) between you and the opposing party or relevant third parties.
- Evidence of incidents: police reports, 911 calls, medical records, photos, videos, witness contact information and brief statements.
- Employment docs: employer letters, termination notices, job offer letters, proof of income loss if relevant.
- Any previous court orders or divorce/separation documents.

C — Typical steps and timeline (what to expect)
- Preliminary hearing (first substantive court appearance)
  - Purpose: often to set issues, address temporary orders, scheduling, bailiff matters, or immediate relief. It may resolve some temporary issues but not full trial merits.
  - Possible outcomes: scheduling orders, temporary possession/custody/support orders, continuance, or referral to mediation.
- Discovery phase
  - Exchange of documents, written questions (interrogatories), and depositions. This can take weeks to months.
- Motions
  - Pretrial motions to exclude evidence, for temporary relief (support, restraining orders), or to dismiss claims.
- Settlement negotiations / mediation
  - Courts often encourage or require mediation. Many cases settle before trial.
- Trial preparation
  - Final witness lists, exhibits, trial briefs, pretrial conferences.
- Trial
  - Evidence, witnesses, judge (or jury if applicable) decides.
- Post-trial
  - Enforcement of orders, appeals (if either side appeals), post-judgment motions, compliance monitoring.

Timing: Varies widely. Preliminary hearings can be within days–weeks; discovery and motions can add months. Trials may be months out, depending on court calendar and complexity.

D — Immediate action items before the preliminary hearing
- Schedule a meeting with Robert Kim ASAP to go over the list above.
- Provide your attorney the documents listed in section B (scan/upload if possible).
- Prepare a concise timeline of events (dates, locations, actions) to give to your attorney.
- Create a list of possible witnesses with contact info and a short note on what each can say.
- Save and preserve all communications and evidence now (don’t delete texts or emails).
- Decide whether you want to seek temporary orders (custody, support, exclusion from the home) and ask your attorney to file them if necessary.
- Ask your attorney whether you should appear in court and what you should (and should not) say if called to testify.
- Ask your attorney if any immediate filings (responses, motions, affidavits) should be submitted before the preliminary hearing.

E — Safety, immigration, and special considerations
- If there are any safety concerns for you or your children, tell your attorney immediately and ask about emergency protective orders.
- If the case could affect immigration, ask whether you should avoid any admissions or statements until you and your attorney have discussed immigration consequences.
- If your ex-spouse’s actions could constitute criminal acts (abuse, threats, fraud), discuss whether contacting police or obtaining criminal reports will be helpful.

Would you like me to:
- Draft an email or a short message you can send to Robert Kim listing these questions and attaching the documents checklist?
- Create a one-page printed checklist you can bring to your meeting?

Tell me which of those (or anything else) you want help with next.

