I can help — this is a good time to be organized. Below I’ve put together (A) a prioritized list of questions to ask your attorney (Robert Kim), grouped by topic with suggested wording; (B) a checklist of documents and notes to bring to your next meeting; (C) a concise, typical timeline of next steps (general — courts and cases vary); and (D) practical next actions you can take now. If you want, I can also draft an email or a one‑page checklist you can print and bring to the meeting.

Before that, one quick clarification: are the “immigration status review” matter and the “proceedings at housing court” the same case, or are these two separate matters (one immigration, one housing/civil)? Also, when is the preliminary hearing scheduled? That helps me tailor the timeline and questions.

A. Questions to ask your attorney (grouped and with suggested wording)
- Case basics and scope
  - “Can you explain the legal claims and issues in plain language and how the immigration review and housing/court matter interact (if at all)?”
  - “What are my immediate legal rights and protections in each forum (immigration, housing, family/custody)?”

- Process & timeline
  - “What is the purpose of the preliminary hearing in my case, and what should I expect will happen there?”
  - “What are the next procedural steps after the preliminary hearing? Rough timeline to trial if it goes that far?”
  - “What deadlines should I be aware of (discovery, motions, filing deadlines) in Maricopa County?”

- Strategy & likely outcomes
  - “What are the realistic outcomes for my case (best, likely, worst)? What factors most affect those outcomes?”
  - “Is settlement/mediation a realistic option? What would you recommend and what would a settlement typically include?”

- Immigration-specific (if immigration proceeding exists)
  - “Will anything in the housing/family proceedings affect my immigration status (e.g., deportation risk, eligibility for relief, public charge, reporting requirements)?”
  - “If immigration authorities become involved, what should I do and will you coordinate with an immigration specialist if needed?”
  - “Do any disclosures or admissions in court create immigration consequences?”

- Custody, children, and financial issues
  - “How will custody and support issues be handled? What factors does the court weigh in Maricopa County?”
  - “Should I request temporary custody/support orders at the preliminary hearing?”
  - “How are marital/household assets treated if there is property or financial disputes? What documents do you need to value these?”

- Evidence and witnesses
  - “What evidence is most important to prove my position? What is your plan to gather and preserve that evidence?”
  - “Which witnesses should I identify and how should I prepare them?”
  - “Should we subpoena records (bank, phone, employer)? If yes, what’s the timeline and cost?”

- Procedural protections and motions
  - “Are there immediate protective orders, restraining orders, or emergency relief I should seek or be concerned about?”
  - “Are there routine pretrial motions you recommend (motion to dismiss, motion in limine, discovery motions)?”

- Costs, fees, and communication
  - “What are your fees and costs I should budget for (court fees, process servers, expert witnesses)?”
  - “How will you keep me updated? Best way/time to contact you and expected response times?”
  - “If I can’t make a hearing, how do we request continuances and how often are they granted?”

- Trial and appeals
  - “If we go to trial, how long will it last and what is the trial process here? What should I do to prepare?”
  - “What are my appeal rights if the outcome is unfavorable? Deadlines to preserve those rights?”

- Authorization & coordination
  - “Do you need my written authorization to communicate with other counsel, child services, or immigration counsel?”
  - “Do you recommend bringing in or consult with a specialist (immigration attorney, family law expert, forensic accountant)?”

B. Documents & information to bring to your attorney meeting
- Identity & immigration: passport, green card, visa documents, notices to appear, any USCIS/EOIR letters
- All court papers you’ve received or filed (summons, petitions, notices, complaints, temporary orders)
- Timeline of events: short, dated chronology (who did what and when)
- Communications: emails, texts, social media messages, letters, voicemails with dates
- Housing documents: lease, eviction notices, rent receipts, notices from landlord, utility bills
- Financial documents: bank statements (last 6–12 months), pay stubs, tax returns, credit card statements, mortgage docs
- Custody/child documents: birth certificates, school records, medical records, proof of childcare expenses, existing custody orders
- Photos, videos, recordings, or other evidence relevant to housing/custody allegations
- Witness list with contact info and short note on what each witness would say
- Any police reports, restraining orders, or criminal records
- Names and contact info for landlord, ex‑spouse, employer(s), neighbors, and anyone who can corroborate events

C. Typical short timeline and what happens at each stage (general)
- Pre-hearing preparation (now — immediately before hearing): gather documents, prepare witness list, discuss strategy with attorney
- Preliminary hearing: often for status setting, temporary orders, emergency relief, scheduling discovery; not always a full evidentiary trial
- Discovery / information exchange: request/produce documents, take depositions (if relevant)
- Settlement/mediation attempts: many civil/family disputes try mediation before trial
- Pretrial motions and conferences: narrow issues, decide evidence disputes
- Trial: evidence presented, witnesses, judge (or jury if applicable) makes decision
- Post-trial: enforcement of orders, possible appeals or motions for reconsideration

Note: Immigration proceedings follow a different federal process (if your case is in immigration court), with hearings before an immigration judge and different relief options and deadlines.

D. Practical next steps you can do now
- Create a one-page chronology of events (dates, one-sentence description each) to give to Robert Kim.
- Organize and scan the documents above into labeled PDF files.
- Compile witness contact info and short summaries of what they will say.
- Decide your priorities (e.g., keep the children together, keep the home, limit financial exposure) so your attorney can negotiate accordingly.
- Ask your attorney for a written plan and a list of deadlines after your next meeting.

