I can help you get organized and ready for the preliminary hearing and next steps. Below I’ve put together (1) a short prioritized list of the most important questions to ask your attorney, (2) more detailed topic-based questions you can use during your meeting with Robert Kim, (3) a typical timeline/what to expect in housing-family cases that may proceed to trial, (4) a practical document and evidence checklist, and (5) some prep tips and next actions. If you want, I can also draft a concise email or agenda you can send to Robert with these questions.

Important note: I’m giving general information and a checklist to prepare questions. For legal advice specific to your situation (especially on immigration consequences), rely on your attorney and — for immigration-specific issues — consider also consulting an immigration attorney.

1) Top priority questions to ask Robert Kim (quick list)
- What is my current legal status in this case and what will happen at the preliminary hearing?
- How could this housing/court proceeding affect my immigration status? Should I see an immigration lawyer?
- What immediate relief can we ask for (temporary orders, possession, custody, financial support)?
- What are the most important documents and evidence I should bring/produce now?
- What is the likely timeline from preliminary hearing to trial (or settlement)?
- What is your strategy and what outcomes should I realistically expect?
- What are the deadlines I must meet (filings, discovery, court appearances)?
- How do we handle communications or contact with my ex-spouse to protect my case and my immigration position?
- What are your fees, expected additional costs, and billing/contact protocol?

2) Detailed questions to cover by topic
Case status & process
- What exactly will be decided at the preliminary hearing? Will evidence be admitted or is it just scheduling?
- What issues are likely to be resolved now (possession of housing, temporary custody, emergency relief)?
- Will we have a settlement conference or mediation before trial?
- What motions do you expect we’ll need to file and when?

Immigration implications
- Could court orders (custody, child support, restraining orders, or a judgment) affect my immigration case, visa/adjustment, or removal proceedings?
- Which findings or orders are most likely to trigger immigration consequences?
- Do you recommend I speak with an immigration specialist now? If so, can you refer one?

Custody & children (if applicable)
- Can we get temporary custody or a parenting plan before trial?
- What evidence best supports my custody position (school records, childcare records, text logs)?
- How should we document parenting time, visitation problems, or safety concerns?

Financial & property issues
- How will assets, debts, rent/mortgage responsibilities, or support be handled temporarily?
- Can we ask for temporary spousal support, child support, or payment of housing costs during the process?

Housing-court–specific questions
- Are there local Maricopa County housing-court procedures I must follow (e-filing portal, forms, timelines)?
- If the dispute is about possession (eviction/lockout), what emergency relief can we pursue?
- Will the court issue any temporary orders about who stays in the residence?

Evidence & witnesses
- Which documents are most persuasive for the preliminary hearing vs. trial?
- Do we need witness affidavits now? Will we subpoena witnesses?
- How should I preserve electronic evidence (texts, emails, social media, photos)?

Discovery & pretrial
- What discovery can we request and what is the schedule?
- Are there specific motions you expect to file (temporary restraining order, discovery motions, motions in limine)?
- How long before trial will we exchange evidence and witness lists?

Strategy & settlement
- What settlement options should I consider?
- What are the pros/cons of settling now vs. going to trial?
- If mediation is offered, how do we prepare?

Fees, logistics & communication
- How will you bill for court appearances, filings, and negotiations? What’s the estimate to trial?
- What is the best way and expected turnaround for updates/questions between now and the hearing?
- What do I need to do immediately to avoid missing deadlines?

3) Typical timeline & what to expect (general)
- Preliminary hearing / initial appearance: court may set temporary orders (possession, temporary support, parenting time), set a scheduling order, and set dates for discovery/mediation/pretrial.
- Discovery period: exchange documents, request records, take depositions if needed (timeline varies).
- Motions & pretrial: parties file motions to resolve narrow disputes or requests for temporary relief; courts may hold hearings on those.
- Settlement/mediation: many family/housing matters settle before trial via mediation or negotiation.
- Trial: if no settlement, trial is scheduled; both sides present evidence and witnesses; the judge issues a final order.
- Post-trial: enforcement or appeals can follow; for immigration consequences, orders may be used in separate immigration proceedings.

Timing varies widely: a preliminary hearing could be days to weeks away; trial could be months out depending on court backlog and the complexity of your case. Ask Robert for a case-specific timeline.

4) Document & evidence checklist (gather these now)
Identity & contact
- Copies of passport, visa, green card (if any), and any immigration paperwork.
- Your contact info and your attorney’s contact info.

Housing & financial
- Lease, mortgage statements, rent receipts.
- Utility bills showing residency.
- Bank statements (last 6–12 months).
- Pay stubs, tax returns, proof of income.
- Bills or invoices relevant to the dispute.

Custody & children
- Birth certificates, school records, medical records.
- Parenting time calendar (detailed dates/times of custody and any incidents).
- Childcare or school communication showing presence/absence.

Communications & incidents
- Text messages, emails, social media messages related to the dispute (preserve originals/screenshots with timestamps).
- Photos, videos, or audio relevant to housing condition, threats, or incidents.
- Police reports, medical records, protective orders.

Legal documents
- Any previous court orders (temporary or final), petitions, complaints, summonses.
- Correspondence with your ex-spouse or their counsel.
- Notes of any meetings or conversations (dates, times, content).

Witnesses
- Names and contact info of witnesses (friends, neighbors, childcare providers, co-workers) and short notes on what they can testify about.

5) Practical prep tips & immediate next steps
- Set a meeting with Robert specifically to cover the top-priority questions above; bring this checklist.
- Ask Robert for a short written timeline and a list of immediate deadlines.
- Organize documents in a folder (physical and a secure digital folder). Label and create a short index.
- Preserve evidence: save originals and make dated copies/screenshots; don’t delete messages.
- Avoid posting about the case on social media; avoid direct confrontation with the opposing party.
- If there are safety concerns, raise them immediately (police report, ask about TROs).
- Confirm the court location, parking, arrival time, dress code, and whether you must appear in person or can appear remotely.
- Practice a short, factual statement of your position for the preliminary hearing (ask your attorney to help refine it).

6) Offer to help
- If you’d like, I can:
  - Draft an email or agenda to send to Robert Kim with the top questions.
  - Convert this checklist into a one-page “pre-hearing packet” you can print and bring to the meeting.
  - Help you make a prioritized document list based on the items you already have.

