Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Good — this is a lot to manage. Below I’ve put together a focused, practical plan you can bring to your meeting with Robert Kim (or use in an email). It’s organized so you can prioritize the most important questions and arrive with the documents and a clear idea of next steps.

Top things to ask first (prioritized)
- What exactly will happen at the preliminary hearing in this case? (What issues will the judge decide/consider at that hearing?)
- How — if at all — could the housing court outcome affect my immigration status? Are there immigration risks I need to address now?
- What are my short-term protections and immediate actions I should take before the hearing (temporary orders, evidence preservation, safety steps)?
- What possible outcomes should I realistically expect (dismissal, settlement, temporary orders, trial) and how likely are each?
- What deadlines and filings do I need to meet now? Who will file them and when?
- What is our strategy for the preliminary hearing and, if necessary, for trial?
- What are the estimated costs (legal fees, expert witness costs, filing fees) and billing/communication preferences?
- Should I consult an immigration specialist as well, or can you cover immigration consequences in this representation?

Detailed question list by topic

Immigration consequences
- Could any ruling or findings in housing court trigger immigration consequences (removal, inadmissibility, impact on applications/adjustments)?
- If there are allegations of abuse or criminal conduct, how would that affect my immigration options (e.g., VAWA, U visa, or other relief)?
- Are there steps we should take now to protect my immigration status (notifications, separate filings)?
- Do you recommend I get a dedicated immigration attorney to coordinate with you?

Housing court / procedural questions
- What is the legal basis of the opposing party’s claims and our defenses?
- What specifically will the judge consider at the preliminary hearing? (e.g., jurisdiction, temporary possession, preliminary injunction, setting a trial date)
- What evidence or proof does the other side need to meet their burden? What do we need to prove?
- What are the likely next procedural steps and typical timeline in Maricopa County for this type of case?
- Are there local court rules or practices in Maricopa County I should know about (filing formats, timelines, in-person vs remote hearings)?

Custody & family-related issues
- How do custody issues intersect with these housing court proceedings? Should family court matters be handled separately?
- If custody or child-support questions are raised, what steps should we take to protect my parental rights?
- Can we request temporary custody/support orders now if needed?

Financial questions
- How will financial matters (income, assets, debts) be addressed in this case?
- Are there immediate steps to protect funds or property (temporary restraining orders, asset preservation)?
- How should I document financial support, expenses, and contributions related to the home or children?

Evidence & witnesses
- What documents and evidence will be most important for the preliminary hearing and trial?
- Who should be witnesses (neighbors, friends, professionals)? How do we subpoena them if necessary?
- How should I collect, preserve, and organize electronic evidence (texts, emails, social media, photos, voicemails)?

Discovery, motions & settlement
- What discovery will we request and what responses can we expect from the other side?
- Should we file any immediate motions (temporary orders, emergency relief, discovery motions)?
- Is mediation or settlement likely — when would that happen and how would we prepare?

Practical/logistical
- What do I need to bring to the hearing and how should I dress/behave in court?
- What is your communication policy (email/phone/text), and who is my main contact at the firm?
- What are the next appointments or dates I should note now?

Documents and materials to bring to your attorney meeting or the hearing
- ID, passport, immigration documents (I-94, visas, green card, receipt notices)
- Court papers served to you and any filings you’ve made
- Lease/mortgage documents, rental payment records
- Notices (eviction notices, lockout notices, outstanding demands)
- Marriage certificate, divorce papers, prior custody orders
- Child-related documents: birth certificates, school records, custody agreements
- Financial documents: bank statements, pay stubs, tax returns, bills, proof of payments
- Communications: texts, emails, social media messages, call logs — ideally exported/saved with dates
- Photos, videos, medical records, police reports, witness contact info
- Any receipts or proof of repairs, improvements, or contributions to property
- Timeline of events: a short, dated chronology of key events and interactions with opposing party

What to expect at the preliminary hearing
- Typically limited issues are decided: whether temporary orders or injunctions are needed, scheduling/trial dates, and sometimes whether the case should proceed.
- The judge may hear brief oral argument and review key evidence; detailed evidence is often reserved for trial.
- The judge may set deadlines for discovery and schedule settlement/mediation.
- Be prepared to give concise testimony (if you will testify) and to follow your attorney’s instructions in court.

Typical next steps and timeline (general)
- Immediate: meet with attorney, gather documents, preserve evidence, serve any necessary notices or motions.
- Preliminary hearing (days–weeks): judge sets schedule, rules on temporary matters.
- Discovery phase (weeks–months): exchange documents, take depositions, subpoena witnesses.
- Pre-trial conference / mediation (weeks before trial): parties often try to settle.
- Trial (if not settled): presentation of evidence and witnesses, final decision.
- Post-trial: possible appeals or enforcement actions (child support orders, possession, etc.)
(Note: exact timing depends on the court’s calendar and complexity.)

Things to protect and actions to take now
- Preserve all evidence (don’t delete texts or emails).
- Document everything with dates and short notes.
- Avoid contacting the opposing party directly about disputed issues if advised not to by your attorney.
- If safety is a concern, ask about restraining orders and safety planning.
- Ask your attorney whether to notify any immigration authorities or to consult an immigration specialist.

Sample phrasing you can use in the meeting or email
- “Can you walk me through exactly what will happen at the preliminary hearing and what you plan to ask the judge for?”
- “Please explain how different possible outcomes could affect my immigration status and whether we should consult an immigration specialist.”
- “What documents would be most helpful for my defense, and can you provide a checklist for me to gather?”
- “What is our best and worst-case scenario, and what is the timeline for each?”

Finally — offer I can help with
- If you’d like, I can draft a concise email to Robert Kim summarizing these questions and asking for a meeting or checklist.
- I can also prepare a one-page chronology template to fill out before your appointment.

Would you like me to draft that email to Robert Kim or create the one-page event chronology template you can print and bring to the meeting?

