I can help you get organized. I’m not a lawyer, but I can list focused questions to ask Robert Kim and outline typical next steps so you go into the preliminary hearing prepared. A few quick clarifying questions first (these will help me tailor the list):

- Is the “immigration status review” a separate immigration proceeding, or is it an issue that may be raised in the housing/court matter?  
- What exactly is the housing court case about (eviction, unlawful detainer, restraining order, something else)?  
- Do you already have any custody or support orders in place, or is custody being decided in this same set of proceedings?  
- When is the preliminary hearing scheduled?

If you prefer, I can draft a short email or a one-page question list to send to Robert Kim — say so and give me the attorney’s email or confirm you want a draft to paste into your client portal.

Questions to ask your attorney (grouped by topic)

A. Immediate — the preliminary hearing
- What is the narrow purpose of the preliminary hearing in this case? (scheduling, temporary orders, evidence, bailiff status, etc.)
- What should I expect to happen that day (who speaks, how long, what motions might be made)?
- Do you want me to testify at the preliminary hearing? If so, what will I be asked and how should I prepare?
- Should I bring any specific documents or witnesses to the preliminary hearing?
- Are there any temporary orders we should request (temporary custody, stay of eviction, injunction/PO)?
- Do we have any deadlines (discovery, motions, filing) that will be set at the hearing?

B. Case strategy & likely timeline
- What are the possible paths after the preliminary hearing (settlement/mediation, pretrial conference, trial)?
- How long before the case could go to trial in Maricopa County given this type of matter?
- What is our strategy for settlement vs. going to trial? What are realistic outcomes?
- Are there steps we should take now (e.g., preserve evidence, file motions, subpoenas) to strengthen our position?

C. Immigration-related rights & risks
- Could anything said or agreed in housing court affect my immigration status (e.g., admissions of fact, criminal allegations, public charge, fraud allegations)?
- Should I avoid making certain statements in court or to the other party because of immigration consequences?
- Will this matter be reported to immigration authorities or could it trigger a review of my status?
- Do you recommend an immigration specialist? If so, can you recommend someone or coordinate with them?
- If there are criminal allegations or DV claims, what are my immigration-specific risks (deportation, inadmissibility, impacts on petitions)?

D. Custody, child support, and financial issues
- How will custody decisions be handled in this proceeding versus family court (are we in the right court)?
- Can temporary child custody/support orders be entered at the preliminary hearing?
- How will financial evidence be collected/verified (bank statements, pay stubs, tax returns)?
- If my ex-spouse is seeking support/arrears, what documentation will they need and what defenses do we have?
- Are there steps to protect assets or housing while proceedings are ongoing?

E. Evidence, discovery, and witness planning
- What documents do you need me to gather and by when (texts, emails, bank records, lease, parenting plans, photos)?
- What witness list should we prepare (names, contact info, summary of testimony)?
- Will you issue subpoenas for records or witnesses? How long does that take?
- How will we authenticate electronic evidence (screenshots, messages)?

F. Motions, protective orders, and emergencies
- Should we consider filing a motion for a temporary restraining order, stay, or emergency relief?
- If the other party violates court orders or harasses me, what immediate steps should I take?
- How do we handle requests for confidentiality or sealed records if immigration is involved?

G. Logistics, costs, and communication
- What are the likely fees, court costs, and costs for expert witnesses or investigators?
- How will you communicate updates (email, phone)? What’s the best way to reach you in an emergency?
- What do you need from me this week and what are our next deadlines?

Documents and items to gather (bring to your meeting and to court)
- Government ID, immigration documents, work authorization, passport
- All court filings, notices, summons, and the complaint or petition
- Lease or mortgage documents, eviction notices, payment history
- Bank statements, pay stubs, tax returns, proof of assets and debts
- Child-related documents: birth certificates, school records, medical records, prior custody orders
- Texts, emails, social media messages relevant to the dispute
- Photos, receipts, invoices, proof of payments
- Names & contact info for witnesses and any experts
- A brief written timeline of key events (dates, what happened, who said what)

What typically happens next (general timeline)
- Preliminary hearing: often used to set case management deadlines, ask for temporary orders, and set discovery schedule. It may be short (20–60 minutes) or longer if urgent orders are requested.
- Discovery/motion phase: exchange of documents and depositions (or written interrogatories). Pretrial motions (e.g., motions to dismiss, motions for temporary orders) may be filed.
- Settlement/mediation: many cases try mediation before trial; judge may order it.
- Pretrial conference: final status update and trial readiness check; judge may set trial date.
- Trial: evidence and witness testimony; outcome decided by judge (or jury, depending on case).
- Post-trial: possible appeals or enforcement of orders, and adjustment of immigration-related steps if needed.

Practical tips for the hearing day
- Arrive early, bring multiple copies of documents organized in a binder.
- Dress neatly and act respectfully in court. Turn phones off.
- Keep answers short and fact-based if you testify. Do not volunteer extra information that may have immigration consequences.
- If something unexpected happens, ask for a short recess to confer with your attorney.

Next steps I can do for you
- Draft a concise email or checklist to send to Robert Kim summarizing your concerns and the questions above.  
- Create a one-page “hearing prep sheet” for you to bring to court (timeline + witness list + top 5 points to make).  
- Help you prioritize the documents to collect now.

Which would you like next — a ready-to-send email to Robert Kim, a one-page hearing prep sheet, or help prioritizing documents to gather? Also, answer the clarifying questions above if you want the lists tailored more precisely.

