I can help you get organized and ready for the next steps. Below I’ve prepared (A) a focused list of questions to ask Robert Kim, (B) a practical checklist of documents and evidence to gather, and (C) a plain timeline/next-steps outline you can expect in family/housing/immigration-connected matters. I finish with a couple of quick clarifying questions and an offer to draft an email or one-page summary for your lawyer.

A — Questions to ask your attorney (Robert Kim)
- Case status & immediate next steps
  - What specifically will happen at the preliminary hearing? What should I expect to be asked or to present?
  - What are the deadlines we need to meet now (discovery, motions, response dates, hearings)?
  - Are there any emergency or temporary orders we should seek or oppose now (temporary custody, support, possession of the house)?

- Strategy & outcomes
  - What is our overall strategy—settlement, mediation, or preparing for trial?
  - What are the realistic outcomes we should plan for (short-term and long-term)?
  - What are the strong and weak points of our position?

- Custody & children
  - How will custody and visitation be handled in the short term (temporary orders) and what factors will the court consider?
  - What evidence will be most important for custody (school records, nanny/teacher statements, texts, social media, evidence of domestic violence)?
  - How can we protect the children from unnecessary conflict during the proceedings?

- Financial matters
  - How will child support, spousal support, and division of assets be considered? What documentation will you need?
  - What immediate financial protections can we request (freeze accounts, restraining orders, exclusive use of home)?
  - How do we handle suspected hiding or dissipation of assets?

- Housing court specifics
  - How does the housing-court matter interact with family/custody issues (possession of the home, eviction, lease)?
  - Can housing enforcement be paused while family issues are pending?
  - Do we need to file any specific motions in housing court now?

- Immigration consequences & coordination
  - Will the family/housing outcome be reported or otherwise affect my immigration status?
  - Do you recommend consulting or coordinating with an immigration attorney? If so, can you refer one or coordinate strategy?
  - Are there particular rulings or findings that could trigger immigration issues (fraud, criminal findings, certain types of orders)?

- Evidence & discovery
  - What discovery tools will we use (document requests, subpoenas, depositions)? What is the schedule?
  - Which witnesses should we prepare or subpoena?
  - How should I preserve and produce electronic evidence (texts, emails, social media, phone records)?

- Communications & logistics
  - How will you communicate case updates to me and how quickly can I expect responses?
  - What are your fees and billing structure going forward? Any estimates for trial preparation and trial?
  - Do I need to avoid contacting my ex-spouse or certain people? Should we use a secure method of communication?

B — Documents and evidence checklist (bring these to your next meeting/hearing)
- Identification and case basics
  - Court case number(s), copies of any pleadings filed or served
  - Contact info for your attorney, the opposing party, and any known witnesses

- Custody/children
  - Birth certificates, school records, medical records
  - Calendar of childcare arrangements, visitation history, travel plans
  - Records of childcare payments, daycare, extracurriculars

- Financial
  - Recent pay stubs, W-2s, tax returns (last 2–3 years)
  - Bank statements for all accounts (past 12–24 months)
  - Credit card statements, loan statements, mortgage/lease agreements
  - Retirement account statements, investment account statements
  - Records of transfers between accounts, roommates/household contributions

- Housing-related
  - Lease or mortgage documents, rent receipts, eviction notices, notices to quit
  - Any communication with landlord/ex-spouse about tenancy/possession
  - Utility/maintenance records

- Communications & evidence
  - Relevant emails, text message threads, social-media messages (save as PDF/screenshots)
  - Photos, videos, voice messages relevant to custody/incident dates
  - Any police reports, protection orders, or referral documents

- Other legal or immigration documents
  - Any immigration paperwork (visa/green card/asylum filings, notices from immigration)
  - Criminal records or any prior court orders
  - Prior divorce or custody judgments if applicable

C — Typical next steps and timeline (general overview)
- Immediate/preliminary stage (days–few weeks)
  - Preliminary hearing: judge sets schedule, may hear urgent requests (temporary custody, injunctions).
  - Exchange of initial documents and requests for temporary orders.
  - Consider early settlement/mediation if appropriate.

- Discovery & motion stage (weeks–months)
  - Formal discovery: requests for production, interrogatories, subpoenas for records.
  - Motions: e.g., temporary orders, motions to compel discovery, protective orders.
  - Depositions or witness statements may occur.

- Pretrial & settlement efforts (1–3+ months)
  - Pretrial conferences to narrow issues; mandatory mediation/settlement conferences are common.
  - Parties often reach settlement before trial; if not, final pretrial orders are issued.

- Trial (months+ depending on docket)
  - Trial preparation: exhibit lists, witness prep, subpoenas.
  - Trial date is set; trial can take days to weeks depending on complexity.

Note: Timelines vary widely by county—ask Robert Kim for Maricopa County-specific scheduling and typical calendar times.

Practical tips while proceedings are ongoing
- Preserve everything and don’t delete potentially relevant communications.
- Keep a contemporaneous journal with dates, times, and short descriptions of incidents or interactions.
- Avoid posting about the case on social media.
- Try to limit direct contact with your ex-spouse—communicate via attorney or in writing when possible.
- If you’re worried about immigration, get an immigration attorney involved early.

Would you like me to:
- Draft a concise email you can send to Robert Kim outlining these questions and attaching the checklist? (I can draft it for you.)
- Create a one-page summary or printable checklist to take to the preliminary hearing?
- Help assemble/search specific documents (I can help you make a folder/list)?

Quick clarifying questions so I can tailor things better:
- When is the preliminary hearing scheduled?
- Is your immigration matter tied to removal proceedings, a visa application, or adjustment of status (or are you not sure)?
- Are there children involved? If so, how many and what ages?
- Is the housing-court issue eviction/possession, or something else (e.g., lease dispute)?

Say which of the follow-ups you want (draft email, one-page checklist, or tailored questions for the hearing) and I’ll prepare it.

