- Could any of the court outcomes be reported/used in immigration proceedings or by ICE? If so, how?
- Do I need to get an immigration attorney involved/coordinate with you? If not, should you consult one?
- Can I safely provide documents about my immigration status in this case? Are there confidentiality limits?

Housing-court specifics
- What will be addressed at the preliminary hearing (e.g., jurisdiction, service, temporary orders, eviction timeline)?
- If this is an eviction: can we ask for stay/temporary relief? What grounds are strongest to oppose eviction?
- What temporary orders (stay, possession, rent withholding) can we request now?

Custody & financial matters
- How will custody issues be handled alongside housing/immigration matters? Can they be consolidated or run separately?
- What documents will prove my fitness/ability to care for children (school records, medical, photos, communications)?
- How will financial obligations or arrears be dealt with? Do I need to prepare a financial affidavit?

Evidence & witnesses
- What evidence will matter most here, and how do we present it (affidavits, exhibits, photos, messages)?
- Which witnesses should we call or subpoena? How do we get witness declarations/statements?
- Are there specific formats the court expects for evidence (e.g., date-stamped logs, originals)?

Procedure & court logistics
- What should I expect at the hearing (how long, who appears, what I should say/not say)?
- Will you be the one to appear in court? If you can’t, who will appear in your place?
- How will I be notified of changes or updated dates? What’s the best way to contact you in an emergency?

Costs & timeline
- What are estimated attorney fees, court costs, and other charges (depositions, experts, translators)?
- If it goes to trial, how long is trial likely to take and how long until resolution?
- What are appeal rights and deadlines if the outcome is unfavorable?

Safety & immediate practical concerns
- If there is a safety issue (domestic violence, threats), what immediate protections can we request (temporary orders, injunctions)?
- If children are involved, what temporary custody arrangements can we seek for their protection/stability?

3) Documents and evidence to gather now
- Court papers/notices you’ve received (summons, complaint, notices of hearing)
- Any prior court orders (custody, restraining orders, previous housing or family court orders)
- Immigration documents: passport, visa, I‑94, green card, naturalization paperwork, prior deportation orders, any immigration correspondence
- Lease, rental agreement, proof of rent payments, notices from landlord (eviction notices), emails/texts with landlord
- Bank statements, pay stubs, tax returns, benefit statements (SSI, unemployment, child support), debt statements
- Child documents: birth certificates, school records, medical records, custody agreements, visitation logs
- Communications: emails, texts, social media messages with opposing party, landlord, or witnesses (date/time stamped)
- Photographs, videos, repair invoices, receipts, police reports, medical records
- A written chronology — timeline of relevant events (dates, short descriptions)
- Witness list with contact info and short summary of what each witness will say
- Any other legal filings relevant to your immigration history or any prior criminal matters

4) What to prepare for the hearing and trial (practical steps)
- Prepare a 1-page chronology of key events to hand your attorney.
- Write a short bullet-point list of your goals and priorities (housing stability, children's welfare, immigration preservation).
- Prepare short, factual answers about events (avoid speculation or anger in the courtroom).
- Organize documents in chronological order and create a short index (e.g., Exhibit 1 — Lease; Exhibit 2 — Rent receipts).
- Get witness contact info and written statements if possible.
- Confirm logistics: time to arrive, allowed items in court, any interpreter needs.

5) Typical sequence/timeline (general, Maricopa County may vary)
- Receive notice/summons → preliminary hearing/status conference within days-to-weeks.
- Preliminary hearing: often narrow procedural matters (service, temporary orders, scheduling); sometimes evidence on limited issues.
- If unresolved → case scheduling (trial setting, exchange of evidence, discovery).
- Discovery/pre-trial phase: exchange documents, witness lists, depositions (timeline varies; weeks to months).
- Trial: scheduled based on court calendar; could be weeks-to-months after preliminary hearing.
- Post-trial: possible appeals or enforcement (additional deadlines apply).

Ask Robert for local specifics — Maricopa County calendars and typical timing/availability vary a lot.

6) Communication & case management tips
- Ask how he will keep you updated (phone, email, client portal) and how quickly he’ll respond (48 hours?).
- Ask who else on his team you may contact and when.
- Get a written summary from him after your meeting listing action items and deadlines.
- Keep all originals safe and give copies to your attorney.

7) Safety and immediate risk mitigation
- If you feel in danger or there is risk to you/children, tell your attorney immediately and ask for emergency relief options.
- If housing is unstable, ask about emergency housing resources or social services referrals the firm can recommend.

8) Sample email you can send to Robert Kim (short)
Subject: Documents and questions before preliminary hearing — [Your Name]

Body:
Hi Robert — I want to be prepared for the upcoming preliminary hearing. Can we schedule a short call/meeting? I have attached (or will bring) the following documents: [list top 4–6 items: court notice, lease, immigration ID, custody order, bank statements, chronology]. Please let me know:
- What the preliminary hearing will cover in our case
- What you need from me before the hearing (documents, witnesses)
- Whether we should coordinate with an immigration attorney

Thanks, [Your name] | [best phone number]

If you’d like, I can draft a longer, more detailed email or create the one-page chronology for you from notes you provide. Would you like me to draft that email now using the specific hearing date and the documents you have?

