I can help you get organized for your meetings with Robert Kim and for the upcoming hearings. Below is a focused checklist of questions to ask your attorney, a documents checklist to bring, what the next steps usually look like, and practical prep tips. If you’d like, I can draft an email or a printable one‑page checklist to send to or bring to your lawyer.

Key questions to ask your attorney
- Case overview & timeline
  - What exactly is the current status of each case (immigration review, housing court matter, and any family/custody proceedings)? Are they separate matters or likely to affect each other?
  - What are the next court dates and deadlines I must meet? What happens at the preliminary hearing?
  - What is your recommended timeline and next critical milestones?

- Immigration-specific
  - What are my immigration options or reliefs given these facts? What are the realistic outcomes (best case, likely, worst case)?
  - Will immigration proceedings be affected by the housing or family case outcomes (e.g., judgments, custody/financial orders)?
  - Do I have a right to appointed counsel in this proceeding? If not, what are my representation options and estimated costs?
  - Are there risks of detention, removal, or referral to immigration court? If so, what immediate steps reduce risk?
  - Are there forms or evidence I should file with immigration authorities now (I-485, I-130, U visa, VAWA, etc.)? (Ask your attorney to name the specific relief relevant to you.)

- Housing-court-specific
  - What are the specific claims being made in housing court (eviction, possession, rent arrears, other)?
  - What evidence or documents do you need from me (lease, notices, payments, communications)?
  - What defenses or counterclaims are available? Could this be resolved before trial (settlement/mediation)?
  - If I lose in housing court, what are the immediate consequences (eviction timeline, appeals)?

- Family law (custody/financial) questions
  - How will custody or support claims be handled, and could they be consolidated with other proceedings?
  - How do financial orders (support, property division) affect my immigration case (if at all)?
  - What temporary orders can be sought now (temporary custody, temporary spousal support) before trial?
  - What evidence is most persuasive for custody and financial issues?

- Evidence, witnesses & discovery
  - What documents do you need me to gather and by when?
  - Will we need witness statements or subpoenas? How do we get them served?
  - What is the discovery process/what disclosures are required in this jurisdiction?
  - Which witnesses would you call and how should I prepare them?

- Strategy, settlement & risks
  - What is our strategy for the preliminary hearing and for trial?
  - Are there settlement or mediation options now? What are the tradeoffs?
  - What are the likely costs (fees, expert witnesses, filing fees) and estimate of total expenses if it goes to trial?
  - What are possible collateral consequences (immigration consequences, credit, housing record)?

- Logistics & communication
  - How will we communicate (email, phone, client portal)? What is your response time?
  - Who at the firm will handle which parts of the case?
  - Do you need signed authorizations to obtain records (medical, school, bank)?

Documents and evidence checklist to bring to your meeting/hearing
- Identification & immigration documents: passport(s), visa(s), green card, I-94, any USCIS/immigration notices, A-number, prior filings/responses.
- Court documents: summons, petitions, complaint, notices from housing court, any prior orders (temporary orders, restraining orders).
- Relationship & family documents: marriage certificate, birth certificates for children, prior divorce or custody orders, custody schedules.
- Financial records: pay stubs, tax returns (2–3 years), bank statements, proof of rent payments, arrears ledger, credit statements, benefit letters.
- Housing evidence: lease, eviction or notice-to-vacate, correspondence with landlord/ex-spouse, photos of property condition, receipts for repairs.
- Communications: emails, text messages, messages, social media posts relevant to the dispute, saved in chronological order with dates.
- Witness list & short statements: names, contact info, what they will testify to.
- Any evidence of abuse, threats, or other safety concerns (police reports, medical records).

What typically happens next (general timeline & expectations)
- Preliminary hearing: usually used to set the case schedule, confirm parties, possibly address temporary orders, and schedule pretrial. Bring your key documents and be prepared to state basic facts.
- Discovery & evidence gathering: exchange of documents, subpoenas, depositions or sworn statements if needed. Deadlines will be set for disclosure.
- Pretrial motions and conferences: motions to exclude evidence, motions for temporary orders (custody/support), and settlement discussions or mediation may occur.
- Trial: if unresolved, trial where each side presents witnesses and evidence; judge (or jury, if applicable) issues a ruling.
- Post-trial & appeals: if necessary, post-trial motions or appeals are possible within statutory time limits.
Note: timelines vary by court and case type; ask your lawyer for a case-specific schedule.

Practical preparation tips
- Create a binder (physical or digital) with tabs for each category (court papers, immigration, housing, custody, financial, communications).
- Make a chronological timeline of events (short bullet points with dates). Judges and attorneys rely on timelines.
- Prepare a one-page summary for your lawyer with the top 3 goals and top 3 concerns you have for the case.
- Practice your testimony aloud with your attorney; keep answers short and factual.
- Confirm witness availability well before hearings and arrange written statements if possible.
- Meet filing deadlines — courts are strict about them; ask your attorney to confirm each deadline in writing.

Safety & confidentiality
- If there are safety concerns (domestic violence, threats), tell your attorney immediately so protective steps can be taken and so filings can reflect those concerns.
- Ask your attorney about confidentiality protections and whether certain filings can be sealed or redacted.

Next step I can take for you
- Draft an email to Robert Kim with the questions above (I can tailor it to be short and prioritized).
- Create a one-page printable checklist or a timeline template for you to fill in.
- Help you produce the timeline or organize your documents if you want to share details.

Would you like me to draft a brief email to Robert Kim with the most important questions and attach your documents list? If so, tell me whether you want it concise (top 8 questions) or comprehensive (all items above), and I’ll prepare it.

