I can help you get organized and make the most of your time with Robert Kim. Below is a prioritized set of questions to ask, a checklist of documents and evidence to gather, a plain timeline of what typically happens (prelim hearing → possible trial), and a short list of immediate actions. I’m not your lawyer, so treat this as preparation — confirm details and strategy with Robert Kim.

Top questions to ask your attorney (prioritize these)
- Jurisdiction & scope
  - Which court exactly is handling this (housing court vs. family/immigration/other)? How does this proceeding interact with any immigration case I might have?
  - Is Robert handling both the custody/financial/housing matters and any immigration consequences, or should I retain/coordinate with an immigration specialist?
- Rights & exposure
  - What are my immediate legal rights regarding housing, custody, visitation, and finances while this case proceeds?
  - Could the outcome here affect my immigration status (removal, visa, adjustment opportunities)? If so, how?
- Immediate procedural issues
  - What should I expect at the preliminary hearing? What do you advise I say or not say there?
  - What deadlines (filings, discovery, responses) do I need to meet now?
- Evidence & preparation
  - What documents and evidence should I bring to our next meeting and to court? (see checklist below)
  - Are there specific witnesses or experts we should identify now?
- Strategy & options
  - What are our realistic options (settlement, temporary orders, mediation, going to trial)? Pros/cons of each?
  - What is your recommended strategy for custody and for any financial claims?
- Travel & scheduling
  - I have a planned trip next month — will that conflict with hearings or deadlines? How should I handle travel during the case?
- Costs & logistics
  - What is the estimated cost, retainer, billing method, and likely additional expenses (experts, filing fees)?
  - How will we communicate (email/phone)? How quickly can I expect responses?
- Contingency planning
  - What should I do if the ex-spouse files emergency motions, or if there’s a temporary order I disagree with?
  - What are the grounds and timeline for appealing or modifying orders later?
- Next meeting and tasks
  - What do you want me to do between now and our next meeting/hearing?

Documents and evidence checklist to bring
- Identification and immigration documents: passport, visa/green card/A-number, immigration filings/status notices
- Marriage certificate, separation or divorce papers, any prior family court orders
- Custody/parenting: current custody agreements, school records, childcare schedules, visitation logs, communications about custody (texts, emails)
- Financial: pay stubs, bank statements, tax returns (last 2–3 years), property deeds, mortgage/lease, debt statements, credit card statements, proof of expenses
- Housing: lease/mortgage, eviction notices, rental agreements, notices from landlord, photos of property if relevant
- Evidence of abuse/concerns (if any): police reports, restraining orders, medical records, photos, witness contact info
- Communications: relevant texts, emails, social media posts between you and ex or third parties
- Any prior court filings, summons, service documents, court calendar/notice with hearing date and location
- Notes: timeline of events, key dates, names of witnesses, short written summary of your goals

What typically happens next (simple timeline)
- Preliminary hearing (status/temporary orders)
  - Court may set temporary custody/possession/financial orders, set a schedule for discovery, or schedule a trial
  - Your attorney will often make short oral arguments and request specific temporary relief if needed
- Discovery & pretrial preparation
  - Exchange of documents, depositions (if applicable), subpoenas, witness prep, motions (e.g., to compel evidence)
- Pretrial conferences / settlement talks
  - Courts often require attempts at settlement/mediation before trial
- Trial
  - Presentation of evidence, witness testimony, legal arguments; court issues final orders
- Post-trial steps
  - Enforcement, appeals, modifications if circumstances change

Practical tips and do’s/don’ts
- Do: Gather and back up documents now; create a single folder (digital + paper) labeled with case name/date.
- Do: Keep a secure, contemporaneous log of events around custody/financial issues (dates, times, who said/did what).
- Don’t: Post case details on social media or send anything to your ex without checking with your attorney.
- Don’t: Sign agreements or court forms without reviewing them with Robert first.
- Do: Ask your attorney about whether any temporary orders restrict travel; get permission or court language in writing if travel might affect custody or immigration.
- Do: Keep copies of all filings, mail from the court, and proof of service.

Questions to confirm with your attorney about Maricopa County specifics
- Which courthouse and department is this assigned to (housing, family, or other)?
- Are there local rules or forms we must file? Are there standard local timelines or judiciary preferences to plan for?
- Is remote (video) attendance allowed for any hearings?

Suggested next actions (immediate)
1. Confirm hearing date/time/location and send that to Robert if he doesn’t already have it.
2. Gather the documents above and bring them to your next meeting.
3. Write a short one-page timeline of the facts from your perspective (key dates/events).
4. Do not travel if a hearing is scheduled while you’re away — check with Robert first.
5. Ask Robert to set a clear plan for the preliminary hearing (what he intends to request and what you should expect).

If you want, I can:
- Draft an email to Robert Kim summarizing these questions and asking for a meeting or clarification (I can include your hearing date, if you provide it).
- Produce a printable checklist or one-page timeline template you can fill in before meeting with him.

Would you like me to draft that email to Robert Kim now? If yes, give me the hearing date/time and any specific facts you want included.

