  - Are there steps I should take now to protect my immigration status (documents to gather, filing actions, etc.)?
- Custody & children
  - Are temporary custody or visitation orders likely at the preliminary hearing? Should I request temporary orders?
  - How will custody determinations be made—what factors or evidence matter most?
  - What immediate protections (if needed) exist for children (supervised visitation, emergency orders)?
- Financial issues
  - How are assets, debts, spousal support, and child support handled in this court? Will there be temporary financial orders?
  - What documents do you need to prove income, expenses, assets, and debts?
- Evidence & witnesses
  - What evidence do we already have and what else do we need to collect?
  - Should we subpoena records or call witnesses? If so, who and when?
  - How will evidence be presented at the preliminary hearing vs. trial?
- Motions & litigation mechanics
  - Are there immediate motions we should file (continuance, protective order, motion to compel discovery)?
  - How will discovery work and what are typical timelines/deadlines here?
- Communication & fees
  - How will you communicate updates (email, phone)? Who is the main contact?
  - What are your fees, likely costs to trial, and any additional costs I should expect?
  - What is your strategy if we need expert witnesses or translators—how are they paid?
- Contingency planning
  - If the judge issues an unfavorable temporary order, what are our options to respond/appeal?
  - If new information arises (e.g., an immigration official contacts me), how should I handle it and who should I tell first?

Documents and evidence to bring / collect now
- Identification and immigration documents (passport, visas, green card, I-94, pending immigration filings)
- Court notices, pleadings, complaints, petitions, summonses, and any paperwork from the court
- Any previous orders (temporary custody, protective orders, eviction, restraining orders)
- Timeline of events (dates, places, short notes of what happened)
- Communications: emails, texts, DMs, call logs relevant to the dispute
- Financial documents: pay stubs, tax returns, bank statements, credit card statements, mortgage/lease, bills, receipts
- Proof about children: school records, medical records, daycare statements, schedules, guardianship papers
- Witness list with contact info and short statements of what each witnessed
- Photos, videos, security camera footage, police reports
- Any previous legal filings or records involving your ex-spouse (criminal, civil, family law)

What to expect at/around the preliminary hearing
- The judge may set or confirm charges/claims, set bail/temporary orders, and schedule future dates.
- Often the court sets deadlines for discovery, schedules mediation or settlement conferences, and may issue temporary custody/financial orders.
- Not every factual dispute is resolved at the preliminary hearing—many matters are decided later at trial or by agreement.
- Be prepared to state briefly your position and to request any emergency or temporary relief needed.

Practical steps to do before your meeting/hearing
- Compile the documents above in a single folder (digital and/or physical).
- Create a one-page timeline (chronological bullet points) of the core events.
- List and prioritize 5–7 biggest concerns/outcomes you want (e.g., remain in U.S., custody of children, keep housing, avoid deportation).
- Decide whether you want aggressive litigation or prioritize settlement/mediation options.
- Note any deadlines or upcoming dates and share them with your attorney immediately.
- Avoid contacting the opposing party directly about the case—run all communication through your lawyer.

Rights you should confirm with your lawyer
- Your right to counsel and to ask for time to obtain counsel before proceeding.
- Your right to review and respond to evidence the other side plans to use.
- Your right to request temporary relief and to present witnesses and evidence.
- Your right to request accommodations (translator, disability accommodations).
- Any immigration-specific rights (to counsel in immigration court, to request stays, etc.).

If you want, I can:
- Draft a concise email to Robert Kim listing these questions and asking for a meeting agenda.
- Format a printable checklist or a “top 10 questions” sheet you can bring to the hearing.
- Tailor the questions and documents list if you give me more specifics (e.g., current immigration status, whether there’s a restraining order, whether it’s a divorce/family court case vs. housing court).

