I can help — this is a lot to organize, so below is a focused checklist you can use with your attorney (Robert Kim) and at the preliminary hearing, plus clarifying questions for me so I can tailor it further.

First — a few quick clarifying questions
- Is Robert Kim representing you for both the immigration-status matter and the housing-court matter (or just one of those)?
- Do you have the date for the preliminary hearing? Any deadlines or court filings you've already received?
- When you say “immigration status review,” do you mean a hearing with immigration court (federal) or some review/impact of immigration status as part of the family/housing court case?
- Is there an active family-court custody case separate from the housing court matter?

If you want, I can draft an email to Robert summarizing these questions and asking for a meeting — tell me if you want that and whether to send or save as a draft.

What to ask your attorney (grouped & prioritized)
1. Case overview & next steps
- What is the immediate goal for the preliminary hearing and what does success look like?
- What is the likely timeline from the preliminary hearing to trial (if it goes to trial)?
- Are there any upcoming deadlines I must not miss (discovery, filings, responses)?
- Who on your team will I be working with and how should I contact you between now and the hearing?

2. Rights and legal exposure
- What are my legal rights in this court (housing court) and any separate immigration proceedings?
- Are there immigration consequences I should be worried about from this case (e.g., impact on status, removal, adjustment applications)?
- If there are possible immigration consequences, do you recommend an immigration specialist (co-counsel)?

3. Preliminary hearing specifics
- What will happen at the preliminary hearing (procedural steps, what the judge usually asks)?
- What should I say or not say at that hearing? Should I testify?
- Will you call witnesses or present documents at the preliminary hearing?

4. Evidence & documents
- What documents and evidence do you need from me now? (see checklist below)
- How should I organize and label documents for the court and for you?
- What evidence can the opposing party use against me and how can we counter it?

5. Custody & finances (because you said these are relevant)
- How will custody issues affect this case and what are my rights regarding custody?
- How are financial matters (support, property division, debts) being handled and what evidence will be required?
- Do I need independent financial records or forensic accounting help?

6. Motions, discovery, and witnesses
- What discovery will we request (communications, financial records, rental/lease documents, etc.)?
- Will we request subpoenas for witnesses or documents? Who should we consider calling?
- Are there any protective orders or emergency motions we should file (e.g., temporary custody, restraining order, stay of eviction)?

7. Settlement & alternatives
- Is settlement or mediation a realistic option? What are the pros/cons?
- What would a reasonable settlement look like (custody schedule, financial terms, housing arrangements)?

8. Costs, billing, and logistics
- How are fees and costs structured? What should I budget for going to trial?
- How often will I receive updates and how quickly do you respond to client questions?

9. Post-trial, appeals, and enforcement
- If the outcome is unfavorable, what are our options for appeal and timing?
- How is any court order enforced (eviction procedures, custody enforcement)?

Practical checklist — documents and prep to bring to your attorney and the hearing
- Court notices, summons, and any filings you’ve received (all court documents)
- ID and immigration documents: passport, visa, I-94, green card, any immigration filings/receipts
- Copies of leases, eviction notices, rent payment records, utility bills, housing correspondence
- Communications with your ex-spouse (texts, emails, messaging screenshots) sorted chronologically
- Financial records: bank statements, pay stubs, tax returns, debt statements, proof of support/expenses
- Custody documents: birth certificates, prior custody or protection orders, school records, visitation schedules
- Police reports, 911 calls, medical records (if relevant)
- Witness names, contact info, brief summary of what each can testify to
- Timeline of events (one-page chronological summary you can hand the attorney/court)
- Any prior court judgments/orders involving you and the ex-spouse

What typically happens next (typical workflow / timeline)
- Preliminary hearing: procedural—judge sets the schedule, may rule on temporary orders, and set discovery/trial dates.
- Discovery period: exchange of documents, written questions, depositions (timeline varies, often weeks–months).
- Pre-trial motions / settlement talks / mediation: attempt to resolve before trial; motions to exclude or compel might be filed.
- Trial: presentation of evidence and witnesses; judge or jury decides.
- Post-trial: judgment, orders for custody/support/eviction; possible appeals or enforcement actions.
Timing varies by jurisdiction and case complexity — ask your attorney for their estimate for Maricopa County.

How to use this in the meeting
- Start the meeting by saying: “I want to make sure I understand my rights and the schedule. Here are the top three outcomes I want (e.g., keep my home, protect custody, avoid immigration consequences). What should we prioritize?”
- Bring the one-page timeline and the documents above; hand them to your attorney at the start.
- Ask the attorney to summarize the case status and the next three immediate steps you need to handle.

Safety and immigration note
- If there are any threats, violence, or immediate safety concerns, tell your attorney right away and consider contacting local authorities. For immigration consequences, an immigration attorney should be consulted if Robert Kim isn’t one.

Would you like any of these as:
- A printable one-page checklist for the hearing?
- A drafted email to Robert Kim requesting a meeting and listing these questions (I can draft it for you now and either save it as a draft or send it if you want)?
- A tailored list if you tell me the preliminary hearing date and which courts are involved (immigration vs housing vs family)?

Tell me which of the follow-ups you want and I’ll prepare it.

