Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — I can help you get organized and ready for your meeting/hearing. Before I give details, one quick clarification so I tailor this correctly: when you say “immigration status review” and “proceedings at housing court,” are those the same case or two separate matters? (E.g., an eviction/housing-court dispute plus a separate immigration matter or removal proceeding.) Also, is Robert Kim handling both issues or just the housing/court matter?

Below is a practical, ready-to-use plan: what to ask your attorney, what your rights you should confirm, typical next steps/timeline, and a document checklist to bring. I’m not a lawyer, so treat these as preparation items — your attorney can give legal advice specific to your situation.

1) Key questions to ask your attorney (use these at your meeting or hearing)
- Case basics and immediate status
  - What exactly are the legal claims/charges in this proceeding? (Ask them to summarize what the court will decide.)
  - What is the date/time/location of the preliminary hearing and what should I expect to happen there?
  - Who else is involved (judge, opposing counsel, mediator, case worker)? Are any third parties or agencies involved?

- Jurisdiction and consequences
  - Is this a civil housing case, family/custody, or is there any criminal/immigration court involvement? How do these different courts interact?
  - Could anything that happens in this case affect my immigration status or trigger immigration consequences? If so, should I consult/cooperate with an immigration attorney?
  - If there is a risk to my immigration status, do you (or your firm) coordinate with immigration counsel? Can you recommend someone?

- My rights and protections
  - Do I have a right to appointed counsel in this proceeding? If not, what options exist if I cannot afford private counsel?
  - Do I have the right to an interpreter or reasonable accommodations in court?
  - What rights do I have regarding discovery, requests for documents, witnesses, and evidence?
  - What are my rights regarding custody and visitation while the matter is pending (if children are involved)?

- Strategy and likely outcomes
  - What are the likely outcomes (best case, worst case, most likely)? What is your recommended strategy?
  - Can this be resolved through settlement or mediation instead of trial? What are the pros/cons?
  - If we go to trial, what is our timeline and what steps must we complete beforehand?

- Evidence & witnesses
  - What documents, records, or evidence do you need from me? Which are most important?
  - Who should we plan to call as witnesses (family members, neighbors, professionals)?
  - Are there any documents I should not sign or communications I should avoid?

- Financial & custody specifics
  - How will financial records, income, and assets be evaluated/used in this case?
  - How will child custody/parenting time be handled while the case is pending?
  - Could orders from this court (e.g., a financial judgment) affect my immigration case or other legal rights?

- Process, timing, and logistics
  - What happens at the preliminary hearing vs a pre-trial conference vs trial?
  - How long do we have to prepare? Are there any imminent deadlines (filings, responses)?
  - What filings or motions should we expect to file or respond to?
  - What are court fees, and what are your billing rates / retainer / anticipated costs?

- Communication & next steps
  - How will you keep me informed? Who is the best contact in your office?
  - What immediate steps should I take in the next 24–72 hours?
  - If new evidence or emergency arises, when/how should I reach you?

2) Rights/topics you should confirm specifically with Robert Kim
- Whether you have a right to appointed counsel (especially important if any criminal or removal proceeding is involved — in civil housing court, there’s typically no right to a free lawyer).
- Whether any orders could be enforced against you outside Maricopa County (e.g., judgments).
- Whether any admissions/consents could be used against you in immigration proceedings.
- Your right to review and copy all evidence and file motions (discovery).
- Your right to request continuances or a protective order for sensitive evidence or safety concerns.
- Right to request an interpreter or translation for documents.

3) Typical next steps & timeline (general)
- Preliminary hearing (initial appearance): judge will set issues, schedules, sometimes address temporary relief (possession, orders). Often short.
- Case management/pre-trial conference(s): scheduling, discovery deadlines, settlement/mediation opportunities.
- Discovery & motions: exchange of documents, witness lists, and filing motions (e.g., to exclude evidence, for temporary orders).
- Settlement talks / mediation: many cases resolve before trial — assess offers and risks.
- Trial (if no settlement): evidence, testimony, final judgment or order.
- Post-trial: possible appeals or enforcement of orders (collection, eviction, custody enforcement).
Timing: could be weeks to months depending on court backlog and complexity. Ask your attorney for local Maricopa County timing estimates.

4) Documents & evidence checklist — bring copies (paper and digital if possible)
- IDs and immigration documents (passport, green card, I-94, visa info) — even if separate, bring them.
- Court documents served on you: complaints, notices, summons, petitions, motions, any orders.
- Lease or housing documents, rent records, eviction notices, payment receipts, repair requests/emails/texts (housing context).
- Marriage certificate, divorce decree, separation agreements (if family issues are relevant).
- Financial records: pay stubs, tax returns, bank statements, benefit statements, debts, credit card bills.
- Child records: birth certificates, school records, custody schedules, communication logs about parenting time.
- Communications: emails, text messages, social media messages with dates/times relevant to dispute.
- Photographs, videos, recordings, and witness contact info.
- Any police reports, restraining orders, or prior court orders.

5) Practical steps to do now (before the hearing)
- Gather and organize the documents above into chronological folders.
- Create a one-page timeline of key events with dates and short descriptions.
- Make a list of potential witnesses and short bullet points of what each will say.
