  - How will you communicate case updates (email/phone)? Typical response time?
  - If I need a continuance, how do we request one and what are the chances the court grants it?
- Practical/administrative
  - Are there any immediate protective steps I should take (change locks, safety planning, preserve documents)?
  - Do I need to avoid certain actions (travel, contact with the opposing party) while proceedings are ongoing?
  - Who else on your team will work on this and how do I reach them?

2) Documents and items to bring to your meeting / to have ready
- Court documents and notices (all summonses, complaints, hearing notices)
- Your immigration documents (passport, visas, I-94, green card, receipt notices, any correspondence with USCIS/EOIR)
- Lease/mortgage paperwork, rent payment records, eviction notices, communications with landlord
- Financial records: bank statements, pay stubs, tax returns, debts, shared account info
- Custody/parenting documents: custody orders, child support paperwork, school records, health records, communication logs with ex-spouse
- Communications: texts, emails, social media messages relevant to dispute (preserve originals/screenshots)
- Witness contact info and brief notes on what each witness saw or can testify to
- ID for all parties, and any proof of residence or relationships
- Any prior legal agreements (separation agreements, settlement offers)

3) What typically happens next (short timeline)
- Immediate: meet with attorney, exchange documents, sign retainer/engagement if needed.
- Pre-hearing preparation (days–weeks): attorney reviews documents, identifies witnesses, files/requests any emergency or temporary relief (temporary custody, stays), serves discovery if applicable.
- Preliminary hearing: narrow issues, set schedule, potentially resolve some matters or set trial date. May involve short witness testimony or only lawyer arguments depending on the court.
- Between hearing & trial: discovery, motions (e.g., motions to exclude evidence), settlement negotiations/mediation, more hearings if needed.
- Trial: full presentation of evidence; judge (or jury if applicable) makes final decision.
- Post-trial: appeals or enforcement of orders as needed.

4) What to expect at a preliminary hearing
- Purpose: triage the case — confirm issues, set schedule, decide temporary orders, possibly resolve narrow disputes.
- Format: you or your attorney will present key facts; court may ask questions; opposing side will respond.
- Time: usually short (minutes to a few hours) — rarely a full trial.
- Outcomes: scheduling order, temporary orders (custody/possession/financial), settlement path, or trial date set.

5) Custody and financial matters — points to confirm
- Whether the court can make temporary custody/possession/support orders at the preliminary hearing.
- Whether financial documentation will be required immediately (for temporary support or fee waivers).
- How immigration status may be considered in custody decisions (ask attorney for jurisdiction-specific guidance).

6) Safety and communication planning
- Should you limit direct contact with your ex-spouse? Ask about obtaining no-contact or protective orders if needed.
- Keep copies of all documents, and store them in a secure place (cloud backup + physical).

7) Follow-up tasks for you
- Gather the documents above and create a short timeline of events (bullet list).
- Make a list of witnesses with contact details and short statements.
- Decide what immediate relief you want (e.g., prevent eviction, temporary custody).
- Let your attorney know about any upcoming travel plans that might conflict.

