I can help — this is a good moment to organize everything. First, two quick clarifying questions so my suggestions can be more precise:
1) When you say “immigration status review,” do you mean removal/deportation proceedings, an immigration interview (e.g., adjustment of status), or something else?  
2) Are the housing-court matter and the family/custody/financial issues part of the same case, or are they separate (e.g., housing court eviction and a separate family court case)?

While you answer, here’s a practical, attorney-ready checklist you can use now — questions to ask Robert Kim, documents to gather, what to expect at the preliminary hearing, typical next steps, and immediate actions.

A. Top questions to ask your attorney (prioritize these)
- Case identification & scope
  - Exactly what proceedings are we in (immigration removal, family court, housing court)? Which courts are involved and how do they interact?
  - What are the immediate deadlines and important dates (preliminary hearing, discovery deadlines, motion deadlines, trial date)?
- Strategy & likely outcomes
  - What is our overall strategy? What are the realistic best/worst-case outcomes?
  - How could the outcome affect my immigration status (risk of deportation, impact on green card, admissibility, future petitions)?
- Custody & financial issues
  - How will custody and financial determinations (child support, spousal support, property) be handled? Can a housing or civil judgment be used against me in immigration proceedings?
  - Are there temporary orders we should seek now (temporary custody/possession, emergency support, restraining order)?
- Evidence & witnesses
  - What evidence is most important for our case? What should I collect now?
  - Which witnesses do we need, and how will we secure their statements or testimony?
- Discovery & motions
  - What discovery will we conduct? What documents or subpoenas are needed?
  - Are there motions we should file before the preliminary hearing (motions to dismiss, to continue, for protective order, to exclude evidence)?
- Plea/settlement/alternative resolution
  - Is settlement or mediation an option? What would a settlement include and what are the immigration consequences?
  - If asked to agree to anything at a hearing, what safeguards should I insist on?
- Counsel & experts
  - Given immigration implications, should we also consult or retain an immigration-specialist attorney?
  - Do we need expert witnesses (valuation, mental health, custody evaluators)?
- Costs & logistics
  - Estimated costs and timeline to trial. What are my options if I need more time?
  - How will communications be handled (email/phone)? Who at the firm will be my point person?
- Rights and protections
  - What are my rights at hearings (to speak, to present witnesses, to remain silent if criminal exposure exists)?
  - How does attorney-client privilege apply here? What should I not post on social media?
- Appeals & post-trial
  - If the decision is unfavorable, what are the appeal options and deadlines?

B. Documents and evidence to gather now
- Immigration documents: passport(s), visa, I-94, green card (if any), previous immigration filings, notices from Immigration Court or USCIS.
- Identification and relationship documents: birth certificates, marriage certificate, divorce decree, marriage-related records.
- Custody/child documents: birth certificates for children, school records, medical records, daycare records, photos showing caregiving, any existing custody orders.
- Financial records: recent pay stubs, tax returns (last 2–3 years), bank statements, credit card statements, mortgage/lease, pension or investment statements, proof of expenses.
- Housing documents: lease agreement, eviction notices, communications with landlord, repair requests, receipts for rent payments.
- Communications: texts, emails, social media messages, call logs that are relevant to the dispute.
- Evidence of abuse or threats (if relevant): police reports, restraining orders, photos, medical records.
- Witness list: names, contact info, short note of what each witness will say.
- Anything served or filed already: copies of petitions, complaints, motions, court notices.

C. What to expect at a “preliminary hearing” (civil/family/housing context)
(Note: “preliminary hearing” can mean different things depending on court)
- Common purposes: confirm parties; set/confirm issues in dispute; set schedule for discovery; consider temporary/temporary emergency orders (custody, possession, restraining orders); set or confirm trial date.
- You may be asked about mediation or settlement options.
- The judge may set deadlines for disclosure, expert reports, and pretrial motions.
- It’s usually not the time for a full trial on the merits — more a scheduling/status conference and initial motions.
- Come with your documents organized and a concise timeline of relevant events.

D. Typical next steps after a preliminary hearing
- Discovery phase: exchanging documents, depositions, subpoenas, interrogatories.
- Motions: motions to compel, motions for temporary orders, motions in limine before trial.
- Settlement/mediation: many cases settle before trial — your attorney may recommend mediation.
- Trial preparation: witness preparation, exhibit lists, trial briefs, pretrial conference.
- Trial day(s): testimony, cross-examination, evidence admission, judge or jury decision.
- Post-trial: judgment enforcement, appeals (if applicable), or follow-up immigration filings.

E. Rights to be sure you understand
- Right to effective counsel — ask about experience with immigration consequences and family/housing matters.
- Right to be notified and to receive copies of filings and orders.
- Right to present evidence and witnesses and to challenge opposing evidence.
- In immigration proceedings specifically: there is generally no right to appointed counsel, so you should strongly consider private immigration counsel if exposure exists.
- Right to limit disclosure: ask how orders or filings might be used in immigration proceedings or public records.

F. Immediate actions I recommend you take before meeting the attorney
- Gather the documents listed above into one folder (electronic copies are fine). Create a one-page timeline of key events.
- Make a prioritized list of questions (use the Top Questions above).
- Identify and reach out to potential witnesses to confirm availability.
- Preserve communications—don’t delete texts/emails; take screenshots if needed.
- Avoid posting anything about the case online.
- If you feel unsafe, consider asking your attorney about emergency protective orders.

G. If you want, I can:
- Draft a concise email or call script to Robert Kim listing the top questions and asking for a meeting before the preliminary hearing.
- Create a one-page timeline template for you to fill out with dates and short notes.
- Produce a checklist of documents formatted for printing and bringing to your attorney meeting.

If you tell me whether the immigration matter is removal proceedings vs USCIS status review, and whether housing court and custody matters are the same court or separate, I’ll tailor the questions and a suggested email to Robert Kim and prioritize what to raise at the preliminary hearing. Which would you like me to prepare now?

