Good — I can help you get organized and ready. Below is a focused set of questions to take to Robert Kim (or to use in a meeting), a checklist of documents and evidence to gather, a concise walkthrough of typical next steps (what happens at a preliminary hearing and what usually follows), and a few practical tips to protect your rights. If you want, I can also draft a short email or message to Robert with these questions.

Questions to ask your attorney (grouped by topic)
- Case basics and strategy
  - What is our immediate objective for the preliminary hearing? (e.g., scheduling, temporary orders, settlement opportunity)
  - What is your recommended overall strategy for this matter?
  - What are the realistic possible outcomes (best, likely, worst)?
  - How will you keep me informed about deadlines, next steps, and developments?

- Court, venue, and procedure (Maricopa County specifics)
  - Which court is handling this (housing court / justice court / family court)? Are there local rules or forms I need to know about?
  - Who will appear for the opposing side and what procedures should I expect at the courthouse?
  - Are remote appearances allowed for any hearings?

- Immigration status implications
  - Could anything in these proceedings affect my immigration status or pending immigration applications?
  - Should we coordinate with an immigration specialist? If yes, can you recommend one or collaborate with one?
  - Are there risks from testifying, submitting documents, or settling that could be raised in immigration review?

- Custody, visitation, and child-related issues
  - How will custody/visitation issues be handled right now (temporary orders at the preliminary hearing)?
  - What evidence is needed to support my custody position?
  - How are child support and custody evaluated in this court?

- Financial issues and property/support
  - What financial disclosures will be required and on what timeline?
  - Could temporary spousal or child support be ordered before trial? How is that calculated here?
  - How should we document financial contributions, assets, debts, and payments?

- Evidence, witnesses, and discovery
  - What evidence will be most important to our case (messages, bank records, lease, police reports)?
  - How does discovery work in this court—what can we request and when?
  - Which witnesses should we plan to call and how should I prepare them?

- Settlement, mediation, and alternatives
  - Is mediation or settlement likely or recommended in this case? When is it best to attempt settlement?
  - What are the pros and cons of settling now versus going to trial?

- Logistics, costs, and timeline
  - Estimated timeline to trial if it proceeds (typical scheduling in Maricopa County).
  - Estimated legal costs and billing structure (hourly, retainers, flat fees for hearings).
  - Are there critical deadlines I must meet (forms, disclosures, evidence exchange)?

- Protection and conduct
  - Should I avoid contact with my ex-spouse? If contact is unavoidable, how should I document it?
  - Do I need any protective orders or emergency relief right now?
  - What should I say or not say on social media?

Documents and evidence to gather now
- Identification and immigration documents: passport, visa, I-94, A-number, green card, USCIS receipt notices, pending applications.
- Court papers: complaint/petition, notices, any prior orders, summons, and proof of service.
- Custody/child documentation: birth certificates, school records, medical records, routine schedules, photos showing care.
- Financials: pay stubs, tax returns, bank statements, rent/lease, mortgage statements, utility bills, receipts, credit card statements.
- Communications: emails, text messages, call logs, social media messages, written notices, letters between you and your ex.
- Housing documents: lease, eviction notices, payment records, landlord communications, repair requests, photos of the unit.
- Police reports, 911 calls, restraining order paperwork, medical reports (if any incidents occurred).
- Witness contact info and short notes on what they can attest to.

What typically happens next — simplified timeline
- Preliminary hearing / status conference
  - Purpose: set case schedule, address temporary orders (custody/possession/support), possibly discuss settlement, confirm discovery deadlines.
  - What to expect: short appearances, judge may set evidentiary deadlines or order temporary relief.
- Discovery and motion practice
  - Exchange financial disclosures, request documents, depositions or affidavits as needed.
  - Possible motions (e.g., to compel evidence, requests for temporary orders, protective orders).
- Settlement / mediation attempts
  - Parties frequently try mediation before trial; judge may encourage or order it.
- Trial preparation
  - Finalize witness lists, exhibits, witness prep, pretrial filings.
- Trial
  - Presentation of evidence and witnesses, judge (or jury, if applicable) decides.
- Post-trial and appeals (if needed)
  - Possible post-trial motions or appeals within statutory deadlines.

What to prepare for the preliminary hearing
- Clear summary of your goals and what you want the judge to order temporarily.
- Copies of the most important documents (custody-related, financials, any emergency evidence).
- List of witnesses and short statements about what they will say.
- Stay calm and concise — hearings are often short and procedural.

