Good — I can help you get organized for your meeting with Robert Kim and for the upcoming hearing. Below is a focused, practical checklist you can bring to your attorney meeting and the preliminary hearing, plus a plain explanation of typical next steps and timelines in cases like yours. I’m not your lawyer, so treat this as preparation to get the most out of your attorney’s guidance.

1) Top questions to ask your attorney (Robert Kim)
- Case basics and goals
  - What is our overall strategy and desired outcome for the preliminary hearing and for the case overall?
  - What are my immediate risks and rights in this phase?
- Court-specific questions
  - Which court exactly (housing court, family court, or another) will handle each issue, and how does that affect process and timing in Maricopa County?
  - What should I expect at the preliminary hearing (procedure, length, likely judge questions, evidence required)?
- Temporary orders and emergency relief
  - Can we ask the court for temporary orders now (custody, child support, spousal support, exclusive use of the home, or a protective order)?
  - If I’m at risk of losing housing or immediate access to children, what emergency steps can we take?
- Immigration consequences
  - How could the proceedings affect my immigration status (visa, adjustment of status, naturalization, public charge concerns, deportation risk)?
  - Could my ex-spouse use my immigration status against me? If so, how do we defend or mitigate that?
  - Should I notify immigration counsel or get a joint strategy with an immigration attorney?
- Evidence and witnesses
  - What evidence will be most important (financial records, communications, lease/title, custody records)?
  - Do we need witness statements, expert witnesses, or subpoenas? How will discovery work?
- Settlement vs trial
  - What are the likely settlement options and how often do these cases settle before trial?
  - If it goes to trial, what is the likely timeline, costs, and preparation required?
- Costs and fees
  - What are your fees, likely court costs, and retainer? What happens if we need more work than anticipated?
- Practical next steps
  - What should I do (or stop doing) now? Who should I not contact? How do I document interactions with my ex-spouse?
  - Do I need to appear at the preliminary hearing? What should I wear, bring, and expect?
- Confidentiality and communications
  - How will you communicate updates? Who else will be on the team?
  - Do I have to report anything to any agency (immigration, housing authority, school) now?

2) Documents and evidence to bring to your attorney meeting and to the hearing
- Identification & immigration documents
  - Passport, driver’s license/ID
  - I-94, Green Card, visa stamps, pending USCIS receipts/case numbers, naturalization paperwork
  - Any USCIS or immigration correspondence
- Court and legal documents
  - All court papers you’ve received (summons, petitions, notices, prior orders)
  - Police reports, protection orders, or prior judgments
- Housing documents
  - Lease or rental agreement, mortgage statement, title documents
  - Communications with landlord or ex about the housing (emails, texts)
  - Proof of who pays utilities, rent/mortgage receipts, escrow/HOA info
- Financial records
  - Recent bank statements, pay stubs, tax returns (last 2–3 years), retirement account statements
  - Credit card statements, debts, invoices, asset lists
  - Any documents showing spousal support or child support history
- Custody and child-related documents
  - Children’s birth certificates, school records, medical records, schedules
  - Evidence of parenting time, communications about custody (texts, emails)
  - Any history of domestic incidents or concerns about child safety
- Communications and evidence of misconduct
  - Relevant texts, emails, social media messages, recordings (check local law re: recording)
  - Photographs or other evidence (dates and context)
- Witness info
  - Contact details and short summaries of what each witness can testify about

3) What to expect at a preliminary hearing (plain description)
- Purpose: The preliminary hearing is often to set the case schedule, consider temporary orders (custody, possession of the home, support), and decide if the case should proceed to trial or further fact-finding. In some housing matters it may decide possession or an interim stay.
- Procedure: Short statements by each side, possible testimony or evidence if asked, judge may issue temporary orders or schedule further hearings. It is usually shorter than a trial.
- Your role: Bring organized evidence, be on time, be prepared to briefly state facts if asked, follow attorney guidance about answering the judge and not arguing with the other party in court.
- Possible outcomes: Temporary orders, a scheduling order for discovery and motions, referral to mediation, or continuation to a later hearing/trial.

4) Typical next steps and an approximate timeline (varies by case/court)
- Immediate (days–weeks)
  - Attend preliminary hearing; file or respond to motions; ask for emergency/temporary relief if needed.
  - Start collecting evidence and preserving communications.
- Short term (weeks–2 months)
  - Discovery: exchange documents, written questions (interrogatories), depositions (if used in your case).
  - Mediation or settlement discussions often occur before trial.
- Mid term (2–6 months)
  - Motions on evidence or temporary orders may be heard.
  - Final pretrial conference scheduled.
- Longer term (6+ months)
  - Trial (if not settled) and post-trial motions/appeals if necessary.
Note: timelines in Maricopa County can compress or expand depending on court backlog, case complexity, and whether you request continuances.

5) Immigration-specific concerns to ask/prepare for
- Whether court orders (support, custody, or criminal findings) can affect immigration petitions or removal.
- Whether you should inform USCIS or consult an immigration attorney now.
- Whether working or travel restrictions exist during proceedings.
- How to handle public benefits if you are concerned about “public charge” rules (get immigration counsel advice).
- Steps to protect immigration records and how to handle any subpoenas for immigration documents.

6) Practical steps to take now (before meeting/hearing)
- Confirm hearing date/time/location and bring the notice to your lawyer.
- Make a chronological folder (paper or digital) with copies of all relevant documents.
- Save and back up all communications (texts, emails). Avoid deleting them.
- Limit direct contact with your ex-spouse; when you must communicate, do so in writing and preserve copies.
- If you feel unsafe, call local authorities and tell your attorney immediately.
- Ask your attorney if you should (or can) get an interpreter, childcare, or accommodations for the hearing.
- If you want, I can draft a concise email to Robert Kim listing these questions and attaching a document list.

7) Suggested structure for your meeting with Robert (30–45 minutes)
- Brief factual timeline (3–5 minutes): present key dates, events, and urgent concerns.
- Goals and immediate relief needed (5 minutes).
- Immigration-specific questions and whether to involve an immigration specialist (5–10 minutes).
- Evidence review and next steps (10–15 minutes): what to collect and deadlines.
- Administrative: fees, communication plan, upcoming dates and responsibilities (5 minutes).

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft a one-page list of these questions you can print and bring to the meeting.
- Draft an email to Robert Kim summarizing your concerns and asking for a short preparatory call.
- Help you search your inbox for court notices or communications related to the case (if you want to authorize that).

Which of those next actions would you like me to do now?

