I can help you get organized and prepared. First, a quick clarification: when you say “immigration status review” but also mention “housing court,” can you confirm which court/process this is (immigration court, family court, housing/civil court, or something else)? That affects some procedural details. Meanwhile, below is a focused, practical plan you can use now: a prioritized list of questions to ask Robert Kim, a typical timeline of next steps, a documents checklist, and practical hearing-prep tips. This is general guidance — not legal advice — to help you get the most out of your meeting with your attorney.

1) Priority questions to ask your attorney
- Case fundamentals
  - What exactly is the legal issue before the court (formal charge/claim)? Which court has jurisdiction?
  - What are the immediate deadlines and the date/time/location of the preliminary hearing?
  - What is the standard of proof at the preliminary hearing and what will the judge decide there?

- Immigration-status questions
  - How could the outcome of this case affect my immigration status, including removal, eligibility for relief, or future applications?
  - Are there specific immigration remedies we should consider (e.g., cancellation of removal, U-Visa, VAWA, adjustment of status, asylum, waivers)?
  - Do you recommend coordination with an immigration specialist (if you’re not already one)?

- Custody and financial matters
  - How do custody/child-support and financial claims in this case affect my immigration risks or strategies?
  - If temporary custody/support orders are issued, how can those be enforced or modified later?
  - What evidence will be most persuasive on custody and financial issues?

- Evidence and witnesses
  - What evidence do we already have and what else do we need (documents, bank statements, text messages, emails, photos, receipts)?
  - Who are the key witnesses we should call or subpoena? How will witness testimony be handled at the preliminary hearing?
  - Can we use documents from other proceedings or agencies (school records, medical records, immigration docs)?

- Strategy and likely outcomes
  - What are the realistic best-case and worst-case outcomes at the preliminary hearing and at trial?
  - Do you recommend trying to resolve this by settlement/mediation before trial? What are the pros/cons?
  - If the judge issues temporary orders, how do those affect trial preparation and settlement leverage?

- Procedural and logistical
  - What should I say or not say at the hearing? Will you be present and what parts will you handle?
  - Do I need to prepare a statement? Will I testify?
  - Are interpreters allowed/required, and if so, how do we arrange one?
  - How much will representation cost for hearing + trial (estimate) and billing structure?

- Next steps & communication
  - What should I do between now and the hearing (tasks, evidence gathering, witnesses to line up)?
  - How should I communicate with you (email/phone/text) and anticipated response time?
  - If something urgent arises (new evidence or a change in circumstances), how quickly can we get it before the court?

2) Typical next-step timeline (general)
- Immediate (today — 2 weeks)
  - Confirm hearing date, time, location; confirm you’ll attend and who will represent you.
  - Gather/organize documents and witness contact info; provide them to your attorney.
  - Discuss and sign any necessary authorizations (medical release, financial documents).

- Pre-hearing (2 days — 2 weeks before)
  - File or respond to any motions or declarations your attorney advises.
  - Exchange evidence with opposing counsel if required (discovery).
  - Pre-hearing strategy call/rehearsal with your attorney; prepare statement/testimony.

- Preliminary hearing
  - Judge may rule on probable cause/temporary orders or set dates for trial and discovery.
  - Hearing could result in: dismissal, temporary/ex parte orders, a scheduling order for trial, or settlement/mediation referral.

- Post-hearing (days — months)
  - If case continues: engage in discovery, file motions (e.g., for evidence, custody evaluations), mediation, and trial preparation.
  - If temporary orders issued: comply and gather evidence for modification or enforcement.
  - Consider immigration-focused steps (e.g., filing forms, contacting immigration counsel) based on outcome.

3) Documents & evidence checklist (bring copies and give a set to your attorney)
- Identification & immigration documents: passport, I-94, visa, green card, naturalization documents, notices to appear, any USCIS/ICE correspondence
- Court papers: complaint/petition, notices, previous orders, pleadings, filings
- Relationship records: marriage certificate, divorce decree, custody/parenting plans, separation agreements
- Financial records: pay stubs, tax returns, bank statements, rent/lease agreements, mortgage documents, bills, invoices, proof of support paid/received
- Communications: texts, emails, social-media messages, call logs relevant to disputes
- Child records: school records, medical records, daycare receipts, statements from teachers or providers
- Proof of residence/housing: lease, utility bills, notices from landlord
- Police or incident reports, restraining orders, criminal records if applicable
- Witness list with contact information and brief summary of what each witness can testify to

4) How to prepare for the preliminary hearing
- Create a clear, chronological timeline of events (one page per issue).
- Highlight key pieces of evidence and where they are in your binder.
- Practice a short personal statement (2–3 minutes) summarizing your position, facts, and relief requested.
- Don’t volunteer extra details beyond the facts; run uncertain statements by your attorney first.
- Bring multiple printed sets of documents for the judge, opposing counsel, and your lawyer (if requested).
- Arrive early, dressed appropriately, and turn off your phone. Confirm if courtroom security requires anything.

5) Issues that commonly affect both immigration and family/financial proceedings
- Criminal convictions, protective orders, or allegations of abuse can have immigration consequences — ask how each allegation affects your immigration options.
- Financial obligations (support orders) can affect your ability to meet other legal conditions (bond, fines) or your perceived “good moral character.”
- Custody orders can influence immigration cases when demonstrating ties to the U.S. or the best interests of children (if applying for certain relief).
- Confidentiality and immigration-related documentation: ask how to protect any sensitive immigration materials in family proceedings.

6) Practical communication & next-action checklist to bring to your attorney meeting
- Provide your attorney a one-page summary of goals (immigration status priority, custody preference, financial outcomes).
- Give your attorney the documents checklist above with copies.
- Ask for a written plan: next steps, deadlines, who’s responsible, and an estimate of costs.
- Request your attorney to flag any immediate risks to immigration status so you can take urgent action.

7) Offer to help
- I can draft a concise email or message to Robert Kim listing these questions and attaching the documents checklist, or
- I can format a one-page timeline/talking points you can bring to the meeting, or
- Role-play the attorney meeting so you can practice answering likely questions.

